  <div class="eyebrow">Report</div>
  <h1>Title 15 Amendment Analysis for Spanish Fork</h1>
  <p class="lead">An independent, source-by-source review of proposed &sect;15.3.24.090, data centers with onsite power generation in the I-1 zone, prepared for the Spanish Fork City Council hearing on 18 AUG 2026 and revised on {{ revised }}.</p>
  <div class="mast-meta">
    <span>Prepared for <b>Spanish Fork City Council</b></span>
    <span>Date <b>17 AUG 2026</b></span>
    <span>Revised <b>{{ revised }}</b></span>
    <span>Subject <b>Proposed &sect;15.3.24.090</b></span>
    <span>Council meetings <b>18 AUG 2026 (public hearing, item 7.3, tabled), 01 SEP 2026 (continued two weeks) and 21 SEP 2026 (approved, subject to further changes)</b></span>
  </div>
  <div class="callout co-insight">
    <div class="lbl">What changed since 17 AUG</div>
    <p>The Council tabled the ordinance on 18 AUG, and on 01 SEP voted 4 to 1 to put the decision off another two weeks.<a class="fnref" id="rs90" href="#fs90"><sup>76</sup></a><a class="fnref" id="rs118" href="#fs118"><sup>104</sup></a> At a special meeting on 21 SEP, rescheduled from 15 SEP, it voted to approve the amendment subject to further changes to its text and directed staff to prepare an overlay zone, which would apply only where mapped, to replace it later. The adopted text is not yet posted. <code>SELF-ATTESTED</code><a class="fnref" id="rs149" href="#fs149"><sup>135</sup></a><a class="fnref" id="rs114" href="#fs114"><sup>100</sup></a><a class="fnref" id="rs109" href="#fs109"><sup>95</sup></a> The city republished the ordinance text on 18 AUG with a 0.30&nbsp;lb/MWh emissions standard, a 90-day noise verification test and a facility-wide NO<sub>x</sub> potential-to-emit cap, three things this report had called missing.<a class="fnref" id="rs60" href="#fs60"><sup>51</sup></a> The City Attorney confirmed that future facilities get administrative review with no additional public hearings.<a class="fnref" href="#fs90"><sup>76</sup></a> The air permit is still an Intent to Approve.<a class="fnref" id="rs110" href="#fs110"><sup>96</sup></a> The applicant presented revised commitments on 01 SEP; none is executed.<a class="fnref" id="rs91" href="#fs91"><sup>77</sup></a></p>
    <p><strong>New drafts.</strong> On 16 SEP the city posted two new drafts of the ordinance for the 21 SEP meeting. They lower the cap to 450 MW, allow one data-center site, remove the 65 and 55 dBA noise limits, cap cooling and humidification water, require a development agreement, financial assurance and public annual reports, and drop the cross-reference behind §9.<a class="fnref" href="#fs114"><sup>100</sup></a><a class="fnref" id="rs115" href="#fs115"><sup>101</sup></a><a class="fnref" id="rs116" href="#fs116"><sup>102</sup></a> This revision restates every maximum-case figure at the drafts' 450 MW and one site. Where the report discusses the ordinance's wording, it still describes the 18 AUG text; <a href="#the-21-sep-drafts">the 21 SEP drafts</a> section sets out what changed.</p>
  </div>
</header>
<section class="report-section wrap" id="executive-summary">
  <h2>1. Executive summary</h2>
<p class="lead"><strong>The choice for our community is the proposed data center versus the warehouse using the building's current zoning at its busiest.</strong> Against that alternative, <strong>the proposed data center is the measurably better option for the community: better on truck traffic, diesel exhaust, water, load on the city's power system and tax base, and worse on carbon and on-site heat</strong>. <code>UPDATED</code></p>
<p><strong>Beyond carbon and on-site heat, no drawback to the community was found</strong> in the facility as proposed. Neither is unique to a data center. Many other businesses do the same: vehicles, buildings and industrial facilities all emit heat, parking lots and roofs raise local temperatures, and every large user of natural gas emits carbon.<a class="fnref" id="rs148" href="#fs148"><sup>134</sup></a><a class="fnref" id="rs31" href="#fs31"><sup>25</sup></a> What sets this facility apart is its scale, which §4, §15 and §20 set out in full. Of the local effects, only sound and heat reach a neighbor, and the nearest residential lot line is <strong>about 1,300 ft</strong> away, property line to property line; the nearest house is roughly 1,500 to 1,800 ft.<a class="fnref" id="rs62" href="#fs62"><sup>53</sup></a><a class="fnref" id="rd0" href="#fd0"><sup>43</sup></a> <code>UPDATED</code> §20 now models sound at those homes as well as at a mile. Against the warehouse alternative, truck traffic falls from as many as about 4,200 truck trip ends a day, 128,600 a month, if the building runs as a parcel carrier's sort hub, which the zoning permits today,<a class="fnref" id="rs13" href="#fs13"><sup>11</sup></a><a class="fnref" id="rs117" href="#fs117"><sup>103</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fd0"><sup>43</sup></a> to the 20 to 60 a month, 10 to 30 trucks, the applicant projects <code>APPLICANT ONLY</code>.<a class="fnref" href="#fs91"><sup>77</sup></a> A trip end is one pass through the driveway, so each truck counts twice. Water use likely drops, from the 5 million gallons a year city staff reported to the applicant's 1 to 3 million.<a class="fnref" href="#fs60"><sup>51</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs91"><sup>77</sup></a> The 18 AUG text bars diesel engines for prime power, and the municipal grid serves none of the generation or computing load; only office, maintenance and similar portions may connect.<a class="fnref" href="#fs60"><sup>51</sup></a> The applicant also promised no diesel even for backup, and both 21 SEP drafts now write that into the ordinance: no diesel or dual-fuel engine for standby, backup, emergency or black-start power, except temporary portable equipment during construction or for emergency responders. The later draft goes further, allowing only non-combustion, electrochemical equipment such as fuel cells for primary generation and barring reciprocating engines, turbines and microturbines regardless of fuel.<a class="fnref" href="#fs91"><sup>77</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs115"><sup>101</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs116"><sup>102</sup></a> <code>UPDATED</code> <strong>§6</strong> sets out the alternative use side by side, <strong>§10</strong> the air permit, <strong>§20</strong> sound and heat.</p>
<p><strong>The tax base is a community benefit.</strong> Roughly 90% of the levy goes to entities serving these same residents: <strong>Nebo School District takes 75.2%</strong>, about <strong>$28.8M</strong> in year one against the city's own <strong>$3.8M</strong>, on the applicant's stated $4B investment <code>APPLICANT ONLY</code> at the 2026 approved rate.<a class="fnref" id="rs18" href="#fs18"><sup>15</sup></a><a class="fnref" id="rs68" href="#fs68"><sup>59</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs60"><sup>51</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fd0"><sup>43</sup></a> <code>UPDATED</code> Volition has separately offered <strong>more than $500,000 a year</strong> in direct community contributions, itself <strong>about ten times the city's share of the largest 2025 property tax bill found in Spanish Fork</strong>, the bill on the building Volition would occupy, and says the offer binds only once it is written into an executed agreement.<a class="fnref" id="rs66" href="#fs66"><sup>57</sup></a><a class="fnref" id="rs80" href="#fs80"><sup>73</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs91"><sup>77</sup></a> <strong>§13</strong> carries the arithmetic, the depreciation curve, and the caveat that no development agreement is signed yet, though Volition has so far accepted every substantive suggestion from the community and from staff.</p>
<p><strong>In the 18 AUG text, the performance standards do their job.</strong> Subsection O.6 caps noise at <strong>65 dBA at any property line and 55 dBA at any residential zone boundary, at any hour</strong>, covering generation, storage and cooling equipment, measured under Chapter 9.32 without displacing its enforcement, and requires a sound verification test within 90 days of full operation.<a class="fnref" href="#fs60"><sup>51</sup></a> Evaporative cooling is banned outright, diesel prime power is excluded by definition, and the permitted emission rates are a zoning condition the city can enforce.<a class="fnref" href="#fs60"><sup>51</sup></a> <code>UPDATED</code> Both 21 SEP drafts replace the 65 and 55 dBA limits with Chapter 9.32's existing zone limits and no longer state the evaporative-cooling ban, but they require acoustic modeling at the nearest homes, verification testing, a heat study with monitoring and a water cap.<a class="fnref" href="#fs115"><sup>101</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs116"><sup>102</sup></a> <strong>§8</strong>, <strong>§20</strong> and Table A carry the detail.</p>
<p>One question was worth putting to the Council on 18 AUG, as a point of clarification. The amendment would apply to every applicant in the I-1 zone, at <strong>up to 450 MW per facility</strong> in both 21 SEP drafts.<a class="fnref" href="#fs115"><sup>101</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs116"><sup>102</sup></a> The 18 AUG redline struck <em>"approved as a conditional use"</em> and <em>"conditionally permitted"</em> from every operative clause, while leaving the use heading pointing at <em>"(see §15.3.08.060)"</em>, the Conditional Use Permit section that carries a public hearing and five findings.<a class="fnref" href="#fs60"><sup>51</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs13"><sup>11</sup></a> <strong>So the 18 AUG text read as administrative site plan review, and one stray cross-reference was the only thing suggesting otherwise.</strong> <code>UPDATED</code> Both 21 SEP drafts drop that cross-reference.<a class="fnref" href="#fs115"><sup>101</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs116"><sup>102</sup></a> <strong>§9</strong> sets out the redline in full.</p>
<div class="callout co-note">
  <div class="lbl">The ask, as put for 18 AUG</div>
  <p>Does §15.3.24.090 route these facilities through site plan review, or through the Conditional Use Permit section that the use heading cites?</p>
</div>
<div class="callout co-insight">
  <div class="lbl">Answered on 18 AUG <code>UPDATED</code></div>
  <p>The City Attorney answered it at the meeting: future site plans would be reviewed administratively by staff and the Development Review Committee, at public meetings, with no additional public hearings required. Even if the use required a conditional use permit, he said, the review would remain an administrative action handled by the Planning Commission rather than the City Council.<a class="fnref" href="#fs90"><sup>76</sup></a> The Council then tabled the ordinance to 01 SEP so members could compile revisions.<a class="fnref" href="#fs90"><sup>76</sup></a> The applicant's 01 SEP presentation says every future facility would "return to the public approval process."<a class="fnref" href="#fs91"><sup>77</sup></a> Neither 21 SEP draft adds a public hearing for a new facility, though both require a development agreement and City Council approval of any Material Modification.<a class="fnref" href="#fs115"><sup>101</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs116"><sup>102</sup></a> Nothing has been enacted.</p>
</div>
</section>

<section class="report-section wrap" id="scope-and-sourcing">
  <h2>2. Scope and sourcing</h2>
<div class="callout co-note">
  <div class="lbl">This report is about the ordinance, not one applicant</div>
  <p>The Volition proposal is treated as evidence of how the text performs against a well-documented project. <code>UPDATED</code> <strong>The maximum the 21 SEP drafts permit, 450 MW per generation facility at one site, is stated first wherever it applies.</strong><a class="fnref" href="#fs115"><sup>101</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs116"><sup>102</sup></a> The 393.25 MW in Volition's air permit application is the expected case, and each figure says which basis it uses.<a class="fnref" id="rs1" href="#fs1"><sup>1</sup></a></p>
</div>
<p>This report relies first on primary sources: city, county, state and federal records. Each claim went through an adversarial red-team review, and each cited source was checked individually, with exceptions noted in its entry. The ordinance text analyzed here comes from the 18 AUG Council packet, with page-level citations and color-verified redlines.<a class="fnref" href="#fs60"><sup>51</sup></a> <code>UPDATED</code> The city republished that packet on the afternoon of 18 AUG, 138 pages instead of 131, with a second round of edits to Exhibit A.<a class="fnref" href="#fs60"><sup>51</sup></a> This report was first written against the earlier posting. Where the republished text differs, the section now says which draft a statement describes, and packet page citations refer to the 138-page version. The two 21 SEP drafts come from the special meeting agenda posted 16 SEP.<a class="fnref" href="#fs114"><sup>100</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs115"><sup>101</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs116"><sup>102</sup></a> The record is the city's minutes of the <strong>04 AUG, 18 AUG and 01 SEP Council meetings</strong> (01 SEP still in draft) and the <strong>05 AUG Planning Commission meeting</strong>, the applicant's <strong>04 AUG, 18 AUG and 01 SEP presentations</strong>, and its <strong>15 AUG community meeting</strong>.<a class="fnref" href="#fs60"><sup>51</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs90"><sup>76</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs118"><sup>104</sup></a><a class="fnref" id="rs119" href="#fs119"><sup>105</sup></a><a class="fnref" id="rs113" href="#fs113"><sup>99</sup></a><a class="fnref" id="rs103" href="#fs103"><sup>89</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs91"><sup>77</sup></a> The applicant's own materials from that meeting, its printed two-sided handout<a class="fnref" id="rs5" href="#fs5"><sup>61</sup></a> and the live project page its QR code resolves to,<a class="fnref" id="rs4" href="#fs4"><sup>4</sup></a> are treated as primary sources and audited in full. Parcel, tax, and receptor geometry come from the Utah County Assessor and Treasurer and from computation against the state's parcel polygons.<a class="fnref" id="rs12" href="#fs12"><sup>10</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs66"><sup>57</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs62"><sup>53</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fd0"><sup>43</sup></a> <code>SELF-ATTESTED</code> The author carried out field research directly: on-site visits to comparable operating facilities, in-person interviews with residents, employees and neighboring businesses, and uncalibrated sound level readings, all reported below with their limits stated.<a class="fnref" id="rf1" href="#ff1"><sup>60</sup></a></p>
<p><strong>Everything material here is fully sourced unless otherwise marked.</strong> Where a claim could not be confirmed against a primary document, it is marked <code>UNVERIFIED</code>. Where a physical observation was taken without professionally calibrated instruments, it is marked <code>UNCALIBRATED</code> and treated as corroboration only. Where the applicant has committed to something on the record but no signed instrument exists, it is marked <code>COMMITTED, NOT EXECUTED</code>: the commitment is taken as made in good faith, and nothing is final until the City and Volition sign and date a written agreement. Three markers were added in the {{ revised }} revision: <code>UPDATED</code> marks a passage changed because the record moved after 17 AUG or because this revision corrected it; <code>APPLICANT ONLY</code> marks a figure or commitment that rests only on the applicant's own materials; <code>SELF-ATTESTED</code> marks the author's own account. The <a href="#revision-log">revision log</a> lists every change, and the 17 AUG edition is kept unchanged at <a href="/title-15-amendment-analysis/2026-08-17/">/title-15-amendment-analysis/2026-08-17/</a>.</p>
</section>

<section class="report-section wrap" id="bottom-line">
  <h2>3. Bottom line</h2>
<p class="lead">Volition describes a <strong>250 MW compute load</strong>;<a class="fnref" href="#fs90"><sup>76</sup></a> the air permit it has applied for would authorize <strong>393.25 MW of nameplate</strong>, 1,210 fuel cells at 325 kW each.<a class="fnref" href="#fs1"><sup>1</sup></a><a class="fnref" id="rs3" href="#fs3"><sup>3</sup></a> <strong>Both 21 SEP drafts would authorize up to 450 MW per generation facility at one data-center site citywide</strong>, and count a standalone generation facility as a site. <code>UPDATED</code><a class="fnref" href="#fs115"><sup>101</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs116"><sup>102</sup></a></p>
<p><strong>Under the 18 AUG text, the risk was that this zoning decision would be the only discretionary review any facility built under it would ever receive.</strong> <code>UPDATED</code> Utah's 2025 S.B. 132 created a "closed private generation system" category that appears to describe this design and exempts such systems from regulation as a public utility, though whether its definitions reach a site on the city's own power system is unconfirmed (§11); no interconnection means no utility review.<a class="fnref" id="rs14" href="#fs14"><sup>12</sup></a> DAQ's air permit governs emissions and weighs no siting question.<a class="fnref" href="#fs1"><sup>1</sup></a> Spanish Fork land use is the last discretionary review of where and whether a facility is built. <strong>That made one drafting question worth putting on the record:</strong> the amendment's redline struck conditional-use status from every operative clause but left the use heading citing the Conditional Use Permit section, so the 18 AUG text read as administrative site plan review with one stray cross-reference against it. <strong>§9</strong> sets out the redline. <code>UPDATED</code> On 18 AUG the City Attorney confirmed the administrative reading: staff and the Development Review Committee review future site plans at public meetings, with no additional public hearings, and even a conditional use permit would be an administrative decision by the Planning Commission.<a class="fnref" href="#fs90"><sup>76</sup></a> Both 21 SEP drafts drop the cross-reference and require a recorded development agreement for every facility and City Council approval of any Material Modification.<a class="fnref" href="#fs115"><sup>101</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs116"><sup>102</sup></a></p>
</section>

<section class="report-section wrap" id="the-21-sep-drafts">
  <p class="section-label">Update, 22 SEP 2026</p>
  <h2>The 21 SEP drafts rewrite much of what this report analyzes</h2>
<div class="callout co-insight">
  <div class="lbl">Where things stand, 22 SEP 2026</div>
  <p><strong>The ordinance:</strong> approved on 21 SEP, subject to further changes; the adopted text is not yet posted. The Council tabled it on 18 AUG, on 01 SEP voted 4 to 1 to continue it two weeks, and on 21 SEP, with two new drafts before it, voted to approve the amendment subject to further changes to its text and directed staff to prepare an overlay zone, which would apply only where mapped, to replace it later. <code>SELF-ATTESTED</code><a class="fnref" href="#fs149"><sup>135</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs118"><sup>104</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs114"><sup>100</sup></a></p>
  <p><strong>The air permit:</strong> still an Intent to Approve. The comment period closed 05 SEP, and DAQ's list of issued Approval Orders showed none for Volition's site when checked on 22 SEP 2026.<a class="fnref" id="rs2" href="#fs2"><sup>2</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs110"><sup>96</sup></a></p>
  <p><strong>The development agreement:</strong> not posted, and not on the 21 SEP agenda.<a class="fnref" href="#fs114"><sup>100</sup></a></p>
  <p><strong>The comparison this report rests on holds, and the new drafts strengthen it.</strong> Against the busiest warehouse use the current zoning already allows, the data center is measurably better for the community on truck traffic: up to about 128,600 truck trip ends a month against the applicant's projected 20 to 60 <code>APPLICANT ONLY</code>.<a class="fnref" href="#fs117"><sup>103</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fd0"><sup>43</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs91"><sup>77</sup></a> It is also better on diesel exhaust, water and load on the city's power system. Both drafts write the diesel backup ban, a water cap, financial assurance and public annual reporting into the ordinance, and v.2 limits primary generation to non-combustion, electrochemical equipment such as fuel cells.<a class="fnref" href="#fs115"><sup>101</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs116"><sup>102</sup></a> The data center is worse on carbon and on-site heat; both drafts answer heat with a required study and ongoing monitoring rather than a numeric limit.</p>
</div>
<p class="lead"><code>UPDATED</code> On 16 SEP the city posted the agenda for a special Council meeting on Monday 21 SEP 2026. Item 9.1 is the Title 15 ordinance, with two new drafts: <em>"Title 15 - CC 9-2-26 rev.2 Council edits,"</em> the changes the Council asked for on 01 SEP, and a second draft, v.2, which adds further edits from councilmembers, staff and the applicant.<a class="fnref" href="#fs114"><sup>100</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs115"><sup>101</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs116"><sup>102</sup></a> The agenda records that on 01 SEP the Council "tabled the decision, giving the staff direction to refine the language."<a class="fnref" href="#fs114"><sup>100</sup></a> Neither draft is adopted, and the Council can change either one on 21 SEP.</p>
<p><strong>Labels O.1 to O.13 in the rest of this report refer to the republished 18 AUG text.</strong> The table below sets the later draft, v.2, against it; the numbers in parentheses are v.2's own subsection numbers, 1 to 24. Neither draft says which edits came from whom.</p>
<p class="tbl-n">TABLE A</p>
<div class="tw"><table>
<thead><tr><th><strong>Provision</strong></th><th><strong>18 AUG text (analyzed in this report)</strong> <a class="fnref" href="#fs60"><sup>51</sup></a></th><th><strong>21 SEP draft v.2</strong> <a class="fnref" href="#fs116"><sup>102</sup></a></th><th><strong>What it changes in this report</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td><strong>Capacity</strong></td><td>A higher per-facility cap (O.2)</td><td>50 to 450 MW per facility (2)</td><td>This report now states its maximum case at 450 MW, 14% above Volition's proposed 393.25 MW, which still fits.<a class="fnref" href="#fs1"><sup>1</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fd0"><sup>43</sup></a></td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Citywide limit</strong></td><td>Two data-center sites; standalone generation not counted (O.13)</td><td>One data-center site; a standalone generation facility counts as a site, and splitting a project across parcels cannot avoid the limit (22)</td><td>Closes the 18 AUG gap that left standalone generation out of the quota. This report now states the citywide maximum as one site.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>How approval works</strong></td><td>Use heading reads "Uses Subject to Conditions (see §15.3.08.060)"; site plan review (§9)</td><td>Items 5 and 6 join the I-1 zone's existing "Uses Subject to Conditions" list, which carries no hearing cross-reference, and neither draft mentions conditional use or §15.3.08.060.<a class="fnref" href="#fs13"><sup>11</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs115"><sup>101</sup></a> Site plan review, with a recorded development agreement required (20) and City Council approval for any Material Modification (19)</td><td>The stray cross-reference §9 turned on is gone, so nothing in the text points to a hearing for approval. Council review of later expansions and changes is new.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Generation technology</strong></td><td>Any listed fuel at no more than 0.30 lb NOx/MWh, with a 0.07 Ultra-Low tier; diesel excluded only for prime power</td><td>Only non-combustion, electrochemical equipment such as fuel cells; reciprocating engines, turbines and microturbines may not be used for primary generation, regardless of fuel. No NOx rate is stated. Diesel and dual-fuel engines are also barred for standby, backup, emergency and black-start power, except temporary portable equipment (definitions)</td><td>Stricter than §8 describes on technology and diesel, and the applicant's no-backup-diesel promise is now in the text. The Council-edits draft (rev.2) carries the same diesel backup ban but keeps a fuel-based definition without the non-combustion requirement.<a class="fnref" href="#fs115"><sup>101</sup></a> The numeric NOx tiers discussed in §8, §15 and §23 are gone.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Air quality</strong></td><td>Operate within the demonstrated rates; facility-wide NOx potential to emit held to the major-source threshold (O.7)</td><td>Must hold a DAQ approval order as a minor source and may not operate under a Title V major-source permit; an independent air-quality analysis and AERMOD-type dispersion modeling at sensitive receptors before site plan approval; a post-commissioning verification report within 180 days; independent testing every year (4, 5, 18)</td><td>Keeps the major-source ceiling by another route, and adds the site modeling this report found no one had done.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Electrical isolation</strong></td><td>Permanent isolation; office and similar portions may connect (O.5)</td><td>No interconnection "unless the owner obtains all approvals required under the City's utility code and utility policies"; the ancillary connection may not back up the isolated load (7). The draft's definition of an Onsite Power Generation Facility still describes one "operating in electrical isolation from, and without interconnection to, any electric utility"</td><td>Read together, the definition and subsection 7 keep the data center and everything that serves it on the islanded fuel cells, while ordinary office, maintenance and custodial portions of the site stay on city power, separately metered, so the city keeps that utility revenue. That is how the project has been described from the start, and the only workable arrangement: a 250 MW load is far beyond what a city system that peaks near 90 MW can carry.<a class="fnref" href="#fs60"><sup>51</sup></a> Subsection 7's general interconnection clause would be clearer if it said this in those words. §11's point about the islanded design stands, but the city's lever is now its utility approval rather than a fixed zoning bar.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Noise</strong></td><td>65 dBA at the property line and 55 dBA at a residential zone boundary, at any hour; verification test within 90 days (O.6)</td><td>The 65 and 55 dBA limits are removed: the facility follows Chapter 9.32's existing zone limits and its most-restrictive-limit-at-the-boundary rule. The acoustic analysis must model tonal and low-frequency sound and predict levels at the nearest existing residences; verification testing follows each phase on a schedule set in the site plan or development agreement; independent testing every year, and retesting within 21 days once a complaint is found substantiated (8, 18)</td><td>The strongest numeric standard in the 18 AUG text is gone, so §20's comparison with it no longer applies. The tonal and low-frequency analysis §20 asked for is now required.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Heat</strong></td><td>No thermal standard</td><td>A thermal-impact analysis before site plan approval where offsite thermal effects are reasonably expected, and conditions that "shall include ongoing monitoring at the facility and locations off-site" (10)</td><td>Addresses the gap §8 called the clearest, with a study and monitoring rather than a numeric limit.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Water</strong></td><td>Closed-loop, air-cooled or dry cooling; evaporative and once-through cooling barred; the approved estimate becomes a permit condition (O.9)</td><td>The initial fill of the cooling system and humidification capped at 600,000 gallons a year, then cooling and humidification at 300,000 gallons a year; metered; existing water use allowed for construction, office, business and landscaping; the cap is excused for causes "that are uncontrollable or unforeseeable," such as acts of God, fires or civil disturbance (12)</td><td>A hard water cap is now in the text. Neither draft states the evaporative-cooling ban, although a cap this low rules evaporative cooling out in practice: the applicant told the Council on 04 AUG that older data center technology at this scale could use more than 650 million gallons a year <code>APPLICANT ONLY</code>.<a class="fnref" href="#fs60"><sup>51</sup></a></td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Decommissioning</strong></td><td>"The City may require a decommissioning plan"; no financial assurance (O.11)</td><td>Removal within 12 months of permanent cessation, defined as more than 365 days of inactivity; a plan due before permanent cessation; the City "shall require financial assurance," in a sentence ending "if hazardous materials are determined to be on site at permanent cessation of operations," not to exceed the owner's engineer's estimate, reviewed at least every five years (16)</td><td>The financial assurance §22 recommended is now required, sized by the owner's own estimate.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Successors and reporting</strong></td><td>Not in the text</td><td>Conditions run with the land (17); an annual compliance report that the City makes public (21)</td><td>The one covenant §8 counted as missing, annual public reporting, is now in the text, and the conditions now bind successors.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Enforcement</strong></td><td>Chapter 9.32 and the general code</td><td>Owner self-reporting within 48 hours, a 15-day cure period, a $750 daily civil penalty, and suspension or business-license revocation after a hearing (23)</td><td>New.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Energy storage</strong></td><td>UL 9540, UL 9540A and NFPA 855 named (O.4)</td><td>The City's adopted building, electrical and fire codes, with Fire Marshal review (6)</td><td>The named standards are no longer listed.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p><strong>The two drafts differ in ways that matter.</strong> The Council-edits draft (rev.2) says approval of a maximum capacity does not authorize building or operating all of it at once, requires the decommissioning plan before commercial operation, and requires the development agreement before a building permit. v.2 lets site plan approval authorize the full approved capacity, moves the decommissioning plan to before permanent cessation, caps financial assurance at the owner's estimate, adds the excuse from the water cap, and moves the development agreement to site plan approval. v.2 also adds the combustion exclusion, the dispersion modeling, the yearly independent testing and the daily penalties, and widens the later water cap from humidification alone to cooling and humidification.<a class="fnref" href="#fs115"><sup>101</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs116"><sup>102</sup></a></p>
<p><strong>Not in either draft:</strong> the $500,000 community contribution, a revenue floor, and a numeric heat limit, all of which would belong in the development agreement.<a class="fnref" href="#fs115"><sup>101</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs116"><sup>102</sup></a> The 21 SEP agenda lists no development agreement item.<a class="fnref" href="#fs114"><sup>100</sup></a></p>
<p><strong>How to read the rest of this report now.</strong> Both drafts carry the same 450 MW cap and one-site limit,<a class="fnref" href="#fs115"><sup>101</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs116"><sup>102</sup></a> and every maximum-case figure in this report is stated for one facility at 450 MW. Where a section analyzes the ordinance's wording, such as the 0.30 lb/MWh tier or the 65 and 55 dBA limits, it still describes the 18 AUG text and says so. The Council approved the amendment on 21 SEP subject to further changes to its text, and the report will be checked against the adopted text once it is posted. <code>SELF-ATTESTED</code><a class="fnref" href="#fs149"><sup>135</sup></a></p>
</section>

<section class="report-section wrap" id="what-the-amendment-authorizes-at-its-own-maximum">
  <h2>4. What the amendment authorizes</h2>
<p class="lead"><strong>Both 21 SEP drafts permit "not more than 450 megawatts of nameplate generating capacity, measured per Onsite Power Generation Facility," and no less than 50, at one data-center site citywide.</strong><a class="fnref" href="#fs115"><sup>101</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs116"><sup>102</sup></a> 450 MW is the cap in both drafts the Council acted on, 14% more than the 393.25 MW Volition has applied for.<a class="fnref" href="#fs1"><sup>1</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs60"><sup>51</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fd0"><sup>43</sup></a> <code>UPDATED</code> See <a href="#the-21-sep-drafts">the 21 SEP drafts</a>.</p>
<p><strong>The figures below use Utah DAQ's own method.</strong> DAQE-IN163550001-26 estimates potential NOx, CO, VOC and CO2e emissions as the manufacturer's published rate times full nameplate times 8,760 hours.<a class="fnref" href="#fs1"><sup>1</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fd0"><sup>43</sup></a> Applying that arithmetic at the ordinance's own cap is the state's method at the city's number: an upper bound, as a potential-to-emit estimate is meant to be, not a forecast. <code>UPDATED</code></p>
<div class="callout co-note">
  <div class="lbl">How to read every quantity in this report</div>
  <p>The maximum the 21 SEP drafts authorize, one facility at 450 MW, is stated first, because that is what the Council voted on. <code>UPDATED</code> The expected case, the 393.25 MW in DAQ's Intent to Approve for Volition (no Approval Order has issued), follows in the last column.<a class="fnref" href="#fs1"><sup>1</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs110"><sup>96</sup></a> Neither number is a prediction.</p>
</div>
<p class="tbl-n">TABLE 01</p>
