</tbody></table></div>
<p><code>UPDATED</code> Two rows need their basis stated. <strong>Water:</strong> 250,000 gal a year is the applicant's figure for cooling and humidification make-up, mostly humidification, after a separate one-time loop fill of about 500,000 gal, and the 450 MW column scales it; the applicant's figure for the whole site is about 1 to 3 million gallons a year, with the categories to be set at site plan.<a class="fnref" href="#fs60"><sup>51</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs91"><sup>77</sup></a> <code>APPLICANT ONLY</code> v.2 caps cooling and humidification water at 300,000 gal a year after the first year.<a class="fnref" href="#fs116"><sup>102</sup></a> <strong>Lake Side:</strong> the comparison uses EIA's 2024 and 2025 fuel and generation records, as the next paragraph explains.</p>
<p>One facility at the 450 MW cap would release half to nearly two-thirds of the heat Lake Side Power Plant, the 1,246.5 MW gas plant in Vineyard, rejects at full output, and would use 22 to 27 billion cubic feet of natural gas a year, 8 to 10% of all the natural gas Utah consumed in 2024.<a class="fnref" id="rs93" href="#fs93"><sup>79</sup></a> The comparison is heat for heat, and it is conservative: a data center consumes its own electricity, so all of its fuel energy ends as heat on the site, while Lake Side exports its electricity and rejects the rest, about 1,319 to 1,376 MW-thermal at full output on its 2024 and 2025 EIA fuel and generation records. EIA reports fuel on a higher-heating-value basis and this report states the facility on a lower one, so on a single basis the facility's shares would be higher than printed.<a class="fnref" href="#fs92"><sup>78</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fd0"><sup>43</sup></a> <code>UPDATED</code></p>
  <h3 id="the-two-site-quota-caps-data-center-self-generation-">The one-site limit counts standalone generation</h3>
<p><code>UPDATED</code> Subsection 22 allows one data-center site citywide at any time, counts a standalone generation facility as a site, and bars splitting a data center and its generation across parcels or approvals within one development to avoid the limit.<a class="fnref" href="#fs115"><sup>101</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs116"><sup>102</sup></a> One question survives: the 450 MW cap is still measured per generation facility while the limit counts sites, and neither draft says one site may host only one facility. That is a question for the City Attorney before adoption.</p>
  <h3 id="where-this-could-go-and-where-it-could-not">Where this could go</h3>
<p>Any parcel in the I-1 Light Industrial zone qualifies.<a class="fnref" href="#fs13"><sup>11</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs115"><sup>101</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs116"><sup>102</sup></a> The amendment is citywide in application, not site-specific: the applicant requested a text amendment, not a rezone of one parcel. The next applicant need not be Volition or use Volition's equipment. Under the 18 AUG text it also need not carry any of the six covenants Volition volunteered: it needs only to meet the objective conditions in §15.3.24.090, and it is reviewed administratively.<a class="fnref" href="#fs60"><sup>51</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs90"><sup>76</sup></a> <code>UPDATED</code> Both 21 SEP drafts write several of those covenants into the text; see <a href="#the-21-sep-drafts">the 21 SEP drafts</a>.<a class="fnref" href="#fs115"><sup>101</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs116"><sup>102</sup></a></p>
<p>How far that opens is itself a drafting question. <code>UPDATED</code> In the 18 AUG text the qualifying test is a performance standard, not a technology: generation must be <strong>Controlled-Emission Generation Technology</strong>, fueled by natural gas, renewable natural gas, hydrogen, biogas, propane or blends, emitting <strong>no more than 0.30 lb NOx/MWh</strong>. Equipment at or under <strong>0.07 lb/MWh</strong> is "Ultra-Low Emission" and gets streamlined review; equipment between 0.07 and 0.30 lb/MWh needs Development Review Committee findings of full enclosure, acoustic compliance, and continuous emissions monitoring or annual source testing. <strong>Compression-ignition (diesel or dual-fuel) engines used for prime power are excluded</strong> by definition.<a class="fnref" href="#fs60"><sup>51</sup></a> The 17 AUG edition of this paragraph described the 05 AUG draft, which set a single 0.5 lb/MWh test, excluded reciprocating engines as a class, and closed its list of named technologies by striking "without limitation" and "recuperated microturbines."<a class="fnref" id="rs104" href="#fs104"><sup>90</sup></a> The 18 AUG definition names no technologies at all, so that list question falls away, and spark-ignition gas engines with add-on controls are no longer excluded by name. Neither 21 SEP draft keeps a NOx rate in its definition: v.2 admits only non-combustion electrochemical equipment such as fuel cells, and rev.2 any stationary generation on the listed fuels except compression-ignition engines for prime power.<a class="fnref" href="#fs115"><sup>101</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs116"><sup>102</sup></a></p>
<p>What the 18 AUG text does constrain: generation must be Controlled-Emission Generation Technology, permanently islanded, non-evaporatively cooled, held to 65 dBA at the property line and 55 dBA at any residential boundary at all hours, with annual water use a permit condition, and subject to battery fire-safety review.<a class="fnref" href="#fs60"><sup>51</sup></a> No other Utah jurisdiction in Table 18 imposes any of those.</p>
</section>

<section class="report-section wrap" id="the-local-yardstick-what-500-mw-looks-like-against-e">
  <p class="section-label">5. The local yardstick</p>
  <h2>What 450 MW looks like against everything already here</h2>
<p class="lead">Every existing-source emissions figure here comes from <strong>Utah DAQ's reported emissions inventory</strong>: 256 facility-year records covering 61 Utah County facilities by DAQ ID, 2017 through 2025.<a class="fnref" id="rs11" href="#fs11"><sup>9</sup></a> <strong>Those are reported actual emissions; the data center figures are permit-basis estimates.</strong> <code>UPDATED</code></p>
  <h3 id="every-permitted-air-source-inside-spanish-fork">Every Spanish Fork source in the state's emissions inventory</h3>
<p><strong>There are three in the inventory</strong>, which covers only the sources Utah's reporting rule, R307-150-3, requires to report; smaller permitted sources are not in it. <code>UPDATED</code> Grouping by DAQ facility ID rather than company name collapses two pairs that are the same plant under successive owners: <strong>DAQ_ID 10847</strong>, <em>Sapa Extrusions</em> and <em>Hydro Extrusion USA</em>, one facility whose listed street address changed from 1550 N Kirby Lane to <strong>1550 Hydro Way</strong> in the 2022 record; and <strong>DAQ_ID 11789</strong>, <em>Longview Fibre</em> and <em>WestRock Longview</em>, one facility at 2200 North Main Street with identical coordinates in every year.<a class="fnref" href="#fs11"><sup>9</sup></a></p>
