</tbody></table></div>
<p>Volition as proposed would be permitted for slightly less NOx a year than Provo's Utah Valley Hospital reports, 5.17 tons against 5.44; the data center's figure is a proposed permit estimate and the hospital's is its reported 2023 emissions.<a class="fnref" href="#fs11"><sup>9</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs1"><sup>1</sup></a> On NOx this technology is clean. One facility at the 450 MW cap would come to about 5.9 tons on DAQ's method: a little above the hospital, a third of what the city's aluminum plant reports, and about 1.4 times WestRock's 2023 emissions, which would make it the city's second-largest inventoried NOx source.<a class="fnref" href="#fs11"><sup>9</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fd0"><sup>43</sup></a> <code>UPDATED</code> <em>(Spanish Fork's own 33-bed hospital is not in the inventory, so the Provo hospital is the meaningful hospital comparator.)</em><a class="fnref" href="#fs11"><sup>9</sup></a><a class="fnref" id="rs63" href="#fs63"><sup>54</sup></a></p>
<p>Every power plant inside Spanish Fork today runs on wind, sun or water; the city has no thermal power plant.<a class="fnref" id="rs8" href="#fs8"><sup>6</sup></a><a class="fnref" id="rs9" href="#fs9"><sup>7</sup></a><a class="fnref" id="rs10" href="#fs10"><sup>8</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs11"><sup>9</sup></a> Its neighbors do. UAMPS Nebo in Payson reports about 9× the NOx estimated for one facility at the 450 MW cap, and PacifiCorp's Lake Side in Vineyard, 1,246.5 MW nameplate, reports <strong>176 tons, 30× the 450 MW figure.</strong><a class="fnref" href="#fs11"><sup>9</sup></a><a class="fnref" id="rs49" href="#fs49"><sup>33</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs50"><sup>34</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs92"><sup>78</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fd0"><sup>43</sup></a> <code>UPDATED</code> Lehi, Springville and Payson run their own city plants, which reported 7.39, 3.37 and 0.57 tons of NOx in 2025.<a class="fnref" href="#fs11"><sup>9</sup></a></p>
