<p><code>UPDATED</code> At 75% of nameplate, one facility at the 450 MW cap would use about ten times the city's annual electricity and roughly 62× the Large Commercial class its largest industrial neighbors sit in, and its nameplate would be 17× every generator in the city.<a class="fnref" href="#fd0"><sup>43</sup></a> On the applicant's own basis, 75% of its 250 MW computing load, Volition alone would use about 1,640 GWh a year at the racks, 5.5× the city, before cooling.<a class="fnref" href="#fs60"><sup>51</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fd0"><sup>43</sup></a> <code>APPLICANT ONLY</code> That scale also explains the off-grid design: the city's system cannot carry any version of this facility without new substations and a transmission connection.<a class="fnref" href="#fs60"><sup>51</sup></a> The building's own leasing listing advertises 4,000 A at 480 V with roughly 6 to 8 MW of additional power available, against the 393.25 MW in Volition's air permit application.<a class="fnref" id="rs64" href="#fs64"><sup>55</sup></a></p>
<div class="callout co-note">
  <div class="lbl">Distribution capacity and transmission capacity are different questions</div>
  <p><code>UPDATED</code> City staff estimated that serving a 40 to 50 MW customer through the city's system could take <em>"at least two new substations and a major transmission connection"</em> to existing 138 kV lines, at about $30 million to $40 million.<a class="fnref" href="#fs60"><sup>51</sup></a> <strong>Transmission capacity is a separate matter, and there is a great deal of it nearby.</strong> Federal HIFLD transmission mapping shows <strong>Spanish Fork is a 345 kV hub</strong>: fifteen circuits end at the Spanish Fork substation, four at 345 kV (Camp Williams, Emery, Huntington, Still Mill), two at 138 kV to Carbon and nine at 115 kV, most of them PacifiCorp's and Provo City's, and six Spanish Fork City 115 kV segments run nearby. The substation sits <strong>roughly a mile, 1.5 to 1.6 km, almost due north of the parcel</strong> (bearing 9°).<a class="fnref" id="rs87" href="#fs87"><sup>40</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs62"><sup>53</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fd0"><sup>43</sup></a> <strong>So the accurate statement is narrower than "the grid cannot serve this":</strong> the city's system cannot carry it without a substation and transmission build-out, and transmission-level service would be an entirely different proceeding. <strong>Why that matters is in §11.</strong></p>
</div>
  <h3 id="additional-context">Additional context</h3>
<p><code>UPDATED</code> <strong>NOx, along with particulates and sulfur dioxide, is where this technology wins; carbon, heat and gas are where it does not.</strong> Apart from Lake Side, none of these comparators comes close to the facility's CO2e, heat, or gas use at the cap. Lake Side, the one Utah County facility at comparable thermal scale, burns about 42 to 43 billion cubic feet of gas a year, well above the 22 to 27 billion one facility at the 450 MW cap would use, and rejects more heat than such a facility would release.<a class="fnref" href="#fs92"><sup>78</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs31"><sup>25</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fd0"><sup>43</sup></a></p>
</section>

<section class="report-section wrap" id="what-is-actually-being-proposed">
  <h2>6. What is being proposed</h2>
<p class="lead">Volition Industries, Inc. applied in <strong>JUL 2026</strong> for a Spanish Fork <strong>Title 15 text amendment</strong> permitting data centers with on-site generation in the <strong>I-1 Light Industrial</strong> zone; its proposed text is dated 10 JUL 2026 and its justification statement 16 JUL 2026. <code>UPDATED</code> The target is the existing <strong>1,054,000 sq ft former Fingerhut building</strong> at <strong>4000 E US Highway 6</strong>, marketed since as Western Distribution Center and now as Canyon Logistics Center, at the mouth of Spanish Fork Canyon.<a class="fnref" href="#fs64"><sup>55</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs12"><sup>10</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs104"><sup>90</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs60"><sup>51</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs20"><sup>17</sup></a></p>
  <h3 id="the-warehouse-data">The warehouse data</h3>
