<p>First, the vacancy. 451,792 of 1,054,000 sq ft, about 43% of the building, is being actively marketed, which is what "partly occupied" means in this report and is the closest thing to a measured baseline that exists.<a class="fnref" href="#fs64"><sup>55</sup></a></p>
<p>Second, the listing quantifies the grid constraint from the property's own side. The building is served at 4,000 A, 480 V, 3-phase, roughly 3.3 MVA, and its broker advertises 6 to 8 MW of additional power as available.<a class="fnref" href="#fs64"><sup>55</sup></a> Volition's proposed 393.25 MW is ~50 to 65 times that headroom, and the 21 SEP drafts' 450 MW cap is about 55 to 75 times it.<a class="fnref" href="#fs1"><sup>1</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs115"><sup>101</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs116"><sup>102</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fd0"><sup>43</sup></a> <code>UPDATED</code></p>
<p>Third, the dock doors. A building with dozens of dock-high positions was built for continuous trailer cycling, and the data center would use none of them.<a class="fnref" href="#fs60"><sup>51</sup></a> <code>UPDATED</code></p>
  <h3 id="the-alternative-use-side-by-side">The alternative use, side by side</h3>
<p><strong>The counterfactual is this building in full warehouse use, which the current zoning allows, not an empty field.</strong></p>
