  </figure>
<p>ITE defines a high-cube warehouse as a building that typically has at least 200,000 sq ft and a ceiling height of 24 feet or more.<a class="fnref" href="#fs89"><sup>42</sup></a> This building has 1,054,000 sq ft and 36 ft of clear height, so it is high-cube, and Land Use Code 154 fits it better than the general Land Use Code 150 that warehouse traffic studies default to.<a class="fnref" href="#fs64"><sup>55</sup></a> <code>UPDATED</code> Which high-cube use a buyer would run is unknown, and the zone permits all of them by right as "Trucking and Warehousing," so this revision states each at its highest published ITE rate and takes the busiest, a parcel hub, as the maximum.<a class="fnref" href="#fs13"><sup>11</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs117"><sup>103</sup></a> The building was built as a catalog fulfillment center, and Fingerhut planned for about 60 trucks a day, far fewer than any of these rates; nothing in the zoning holds a future owner to that plan.<a class="fnref" href="#fs19"><sup>16</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs20"><sup>17</sup></a> The building's owner told the Council on 18 AUG that funding had been obtained for a new Highway 6 interchange, and that the owners had considered expanded distribution and manufacturing uses that could bring substantially more truck traffic.<a class="fnref" href="#fs90"><sup>76</sup></a> No state record found for this revision confirms the interchange's funding or schedule. <code>UNVERIFIED</code></p>
<p class="tbl-n">TABLE 09</p>
<div class="tw"><table>
<thead><tr><th><strong>Warehouse use (ITE land use code)</strong></th><th><strong>Highest published truck rate, trip ends per 1,000 sq ft a day</strong></th><th><strong>Truck trip ends a day</strong></th><th><strong>Truck trip ends a month</strong></th><th><strong>Trucks a day, each arriving and leaving</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody>
