<tr><td><strong>LU 157, cold storage</strong>, such as a grocery distribution center</td><td>0.836 <a class="fnref" href="#fs117"><sup>103</sup></a></td><td>881</td><td>26,822</td><td>about 440</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>LU 155, fulfillment center</strong>, the use the building was built for</td><td>0.717; ITE's 11th edition gives 0.19 to 0.23 <a class="fnref" href="#fs117"><sup>103</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs89"><sup>42</sup></a></td><td>756</td><td>23,004</td><td>about 380</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>LU 154, transload and short-term storage</strong></td><td>0.454; ITE's 11th edition gives 0.22 <a class="fnref" href="#fs117"><sup>103</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs89"><sup>42</sup></a></td><td>479</td><td>13,200</td><td>about 240</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>LU 150, general warehousing</strong></td><td>0.56, the top of ITE's range for buildings of 500,000 sq ft or more <a class="fnref" href="#fs89"><sup>42</sup></a></td><td>590</td><td>13,325</td><td>about 295</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p><em>Every rate is published by ITE, the industry's standard source for traffic estimates, and applied to the verified 1,054,000 sq ft; none is a count, because no count exists for this building or for a comparable Utah site. The parcel hub, cold storage, fulfillment and transload rates are the weighted averages in ITE's 2016 high-cube warehouse study; ITE publishes the same weekend as weekday rate for parcel hubs, cold storage and fulfillment centers, so those rows run 30.44 days a month.</em><a class="fnref" href="#fs117"><sup>103</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs88"><sup>41</sup></a> <em>The general warehousing and transload rows use 21.75 weekdays and 8.69 weekend days, with weekend volume scaled by each code's published weekend-to-weekday ratio.</em><a class="fnref" href="#fs88"><sup>41</sup></a> <em>The parcel hub and fulfillment rates each rest on a single site, which ITE judged insufficient to derive a usable rate, and the fulfillment count was taken in December; they are the published maximum, not a forecast.</em><a class="fnref" href="#fs117"><sup>103</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fd0"><sup>43</sup></a></p>
<p><code>UPDATED</code> The 17 AUG edition used 36 dock-high doors as an independent physical check on these rates. That check does not hold. The 36 doors are those listed for the space on the market, the applicant counts 85 bays building-wide, and the listing says more can be added. The door count does not cap the use; floor area does, which is why every figure above is computed from the 1,054,000 sq ft.<a class="fnref" href="#fs64"><sup>55</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs91"><sup>77</sup></a></p>
<div class="callout co-note">
  <div class="lbl">The most the current zoning allows: about 4,200 truck trip ends a day <code>UPDATED</code></div>
  <p>The I-1 zone lists "Trucking and Warehousing" as a permitted use, so a parcel carrier could run this building as a sort hub with no zoning change and no public hearing.<a class="fnref" href="#fs13"><sup>11</sup></a> At ITE's parcel hub rate that is about <strong>4,200 truck trip ends a day, 29,600 a week, 128,600 a month and 1.54 million a year</strong>: roughly 2,100 trucks a day, about a quarter of them tractor-trailers.<a class="fnref" href="#fs117"><sup>103</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fd0"><sup>43</sup></a> By the applicant's projection the data center, with no loading or distribution operations, would generate 10 to 30 trucks a month, 20 to 60 trip ends. <code>APPLICANT ONLY</code><a class="fnref" href="#fs60"><sup>51</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs91"><sup>77</sup></a></p>
</div>
<p><code>APPLICANT ONLY</code> Table 10 is the applicant's own description of the project, from its presentations and testimony. Nothing in it is independently verified except where DAQ's Intent to Approve or the ordinance text is cited.</p>
