<p><strong>The Planning Commission did not endorse this.</strong> It deadlocked. Its review of a text amendment is advisory, so the amendment went to the Council regardless.<a class="fnref" href="#fs119"><sup>105</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs13"><sup>11</sup></a></p>
  <h3 id="two-councilmembers-asked-for-exactly-this-discretion">Two councilmembers raised this question on 04 AUG</h3>
<p>The city's minutes of 04 AUG, printed in draft in the 18 AUG packet and adopted on 18 AUG, record this exchange during the discussion item "Large Power Users, Island Power, and Data Centers," <strong>before</strong> the Volition presentation:<a class="fnref" href="#fs60"><sup>51</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs90"><sup>76</sup></a> <code>UPDATED</code></p>
<div class="callout co-note">
  <div class="lbl">The 04 AUG exchange</div>
  <p><em>"expressed concern that a business could potentially be a permitted use within a zone while still requiring an exceptionally large amount of electricity. In that circumstance, the Council might have no legislative opportunity to evaluate the broader impacts… He suggested that users exceeding certain thresholds might warrant a process similar to a conditional use review."</em> (p. 62) He then clarified that he <em>"had not yet determined that such a requirement was necessary but believed it deserved consideration."</em> (p. 62) A second councilmember <em>"agreed that the concern would likely <strong>require changes to Title 15</strong> if the Council wanted all exceptionally large electrical users to come before it for consideration,"</em> noting that <em>"under the existing code, those businesses would not necessarily require Council review."</em> (p. 62) The city manager <em>"explained that the draft large-load policy itself might not provide the legislative authority [the councilmember] had been seeking and said staff could further examine what type of legislative action might be appropriate."</em> (p. 63)</p>
</div>
<p><strong>The amendment reaching the Council on 18 AUG does not contain that review.</strong> <code>UPDATED</code> The Planning Commission asked for something different, a capacity cap and a two-site limit, and both are in the 18 AUG text.<a class="fnref" href="#fs60"><sup>51</sup></a> The applicant's own 10 JUL text made each facility a conditional use with its own public hearing; staff's 05 AUG redline replaced that with administrative review, and no packet states why.<a class="fnref" href="#fs104"><sup>90</sup></a> On 18 AUG the question was answered in public. The City Attorney said that under this text future site plans receive administrative review by staff and the Development Review Committee, at public meetings, with no additional public hearings, and that <em>"even if the use required a conditional use permit, the review would remain an administrative action handled by the Planning Commission rather than the City Council."</em><a class="fnref" href="#fs90"><sup>76</sup></a> A conditional use permit would not have given the Council the legislative review the 04 AUG exchange was about. The Council then discussed writing more protections into the ordinance or requiring a development agreement of every applicant.<a class="fnref" href="#fs90"><sup>76</sup></a></p>
</section>

<section class="report-section wrap" id="the-ordinance-provision-by-provision">
  <h2>8. The ordinance, provision by provision</h2>
<p class="lead"><code>UPDATED</code> Both 21 SEP drafts replace most of the provisions below; <a href="#the-21-sep-drafts">the 21 SEP drafts</a> section compares them.<a class="fnref" href="#fs115"><sup>101</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs116"><sup>102</sup></a> The 17 AUG edition of this section described two documents: the draft in the 05 AUG Planning Commission packet (pp. 62 to 65) for the performance standards, and the Exhibit A in the original 18 AUG posting for the capacity cap, the quota and the cross-references.<a class="fnref" href="#fs104"><sup>90</sup></a> The Council's republished 18 AUG Exhibit A changed several provisions. Each item below now describes the 18 AUG text, cites its standards by their 18 AUG numbers, O.1 to O.13, and says where it changed.<a class="fnref" href="#fs60"><sup>51</sup></a></p>
<p><strong>Strong in the 18 AUG text:</strong></p>
<ul>
  <li><code>UPDATED</code> <strong>"Controlled-Emission Generation Technology"</strong>, <strong>NOx ≤ 0.30 lb/MWh</strong>, with an "Ultra-Low Emission" tier at <strong>≤ 0.07 lb/MWh</strong> that gets streamlined review. Anything else needs Development Review Committee findings of full enclosure, acoustic compliance, and continuous emissions monitoring or annual source testing (O.3). Compression-ignition (diesel or dual-fuel) engines used for prime power are excluded. Objective, numeric, verifiable.<a class="fnref" href="#fs60"><sup>51</sup></a> The 05 AUG draft offered two routes, CARB certification or a single 0.5 lb/MWh test, and excluded every reciprocating engine.<a class="fnref" href="#fs104"><sup>90</sup></a> The 18 AUG text drops that class exclusion, so spark-ignition gas engines of the kind Novva runs in West Jordan and Joule Capital has permitted in Millard County are no longer excluded by name and would have to demonstrate 0.30 lb/MWh under Enhanced Review.<a class="fnref" href="#fs47"><sup>31</sup></a><a class="fnref" id="rs48" href="#fs48"><sup>32</sup></a> Bloom's ES 6.5 datasheet states <strong>0.003 lb/MWh</strong> of NOx, a <strong>100× margin</strong> under 0.30.<a class="fnref" href="#fs3"><sup>3</sup></a> (0.07 lb/MWh is also the CARB DG <em>certification standard</em>, the ceiling a unit must meet to be certified, not Bloom's rate.)<a class="fnref" id="rs46" href="#fs46"><sup>30</sup></a></li>
  <li><strong>O.6 Noise</strong> is the strongest performance standard in the document. Verbatim: sound <em>"including generation equipment, Energy Storage Systems, and cooling equipment, shall not exceed 65 dBA at any property line of the site, and shall not exceed 55 dBA at the boundary of any residential zone, at any hour, measured in accordance with the procedures of Chapter 9.32 of this Code,"</em> and <em>"Compliance with this subsection does not excuse compliance with Chapter 9.32."</em><a class="fnref" href="#fs60"><sup>51</sup></a> So it is a <strong>continuing</strong> limit, not a design target; it names <strong>cooling equipment</strong>; it adopts a measurement procedure; and it preserves the city's standing enforcement. Against the existing I-1 limit of 80 dBA day / 65 dBA night, a facility here must meet the city's <strong>nighttime</strong> standard around the clock. Volition wrote this.<a class="fnref" href="#fs104"><sup>90</sup></a> <code>UPDATED</code> <strong>The 18 AUG text closed the one omission the 17 AUG edition found</strong>: it now requires sound verification testing by a qualified professional within 90 days of full commercial operation, with results to the Zoning Administrator. On a substantiated complaint the Zoning Administrator <em>"may require"</em> follow-up testing, which is discretion, not a duty.<a class="fnref" href="#fs60"><sup>51</sup></a></li>
  <li><code>UPDATED</code> <strong>O.9 Water Use</strong> requires closed-loop, air-cooled or dry cooling and bars evaporative and once-through cooling for heat rejection, which is the most valuable protection in the document. It also requires an application to include <em>"an annual water use estimate, by category of use"</em> for review by the Public Works Director, and <em>"the approved estimate shall be a condition of the permit."</em> Landscaping is named as a use the subsection does not prohibit.<a class="fnref" href="#fs60"><sup>51</sup></a></li>
  <li><code>UPDATED</code> <strong>O.5</strong>, permanent electrical isolation, no interconnection, parallel operation or export. The text carries a carve-out, first inserted in staff's 05 AUG redline: <em>"Ordinary office, maintenance, custodial, and other similar portions of a facility may be connected to the Spanish Fork City electric system."</em> The last phrase is open-ended, and nothing in the text defines it.<a class="fnref" href="#fs104"><sup>90</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs60"><sup>51</sup></a></li>
  <li><strong>O.7 Air Quality</strong> requires the facility to <em>"at all times operate within the emission rates demonstrated under subsection 3"</em>, making the emission rates a zoning condition the city can enforce, not merely a DAQ matter. <code>UPDATED</code> The 18 AUG text fixed the cross-reference to a subsection C that the renumbered draft no longer had, and it now holds facility-wide NOx potential to emit to the federal nonattainment major-source threshold, by federally enforceable limits in the DAQ approval order.<a class="fnref" href="#fs60"><sup>51</sup></a></li>
  <li><strong>O.4 Energy Storage</strong> requires UL 9540 listing, UL 9540A large-scale fire test data, NFPA 855 compliance, and Fire Marshal review with an emergency response plan before certificate of occupancy.<a class="fnref" href="#fs60"><sup>51</sup></a></li>
</ul>
<div class="callout co-note">
  <div class="lbl">Source</div>
  <p>Exhibit A and the item 7.3 staff memo come from the 18 AUG Council packet as republished that afternoon, 138 pages, retrieved from the Diligent portal.<a class="fnref" href="#fs23"><sup>20</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs60"><sup>51</sup></a> Exhibit A is at pp. 121 to 126 and the staff memo at p. 127. Nothing in it is adopted: the Council tabled the ordinance that night.<a class="fnref" href="#fs90"><sup>76</sup></a> <code>UPDATED</code></p>
</div>
<p><strong>Both 21 SEP drafts carry a capacity ceiling, and it is the number to argue about.</strong> Subsection 2 of each reads "…a minimum of 50 megawatts and not more than 450 megawatts of nameplate generating capacity, measured per Onsite Power Generation Facility."<a class="fnref" href="#fs115"><sup>101</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs116"><sup>102</sup></a> <code>UPDATED</code> <strong>Note the floor as well as the ceiling: nothing under 50 MW qualifies at all</strong>, so every facility the drafts authorize is a large one. §4 works through what that permits at the cap.</p>
<p><strong>Still materially weak in the 18 AUG text:</strong></p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>No heat or thermal standard of any kind.</strong> At <strong>605–742 MW-thermal</strong> for the proposed 393.25 MW, and 692–849 at 450 MW, this remains the clearest substantive gap.<a class="fnref" href="#fd0"><sup>43</sup></a> <code>UPDATED</code> The applicant's 01 SEP presentation promises a third-party, site-specific heat study before operation, with results published; that promise is not in the 18 AUG text.<a class="fnref" href="#fs91"><sup>77</sup></a></li>
  <li><strong>O.11 decommissioning: unchanged.</strong> Exhibit A still requires removal within 12 months of permanent cessation and says only that <em>"The City may require a decommissioning plan as a condition of approval."</em> No bond, no surety, no escrow.<a class="fnref" href="#fs60"><sup>51</sup></a> <code>UPDATED</code> The applicant's 01 SEP presentation promises a restoration plan with a cost estimate refreshed every three years, City step-in and lien rights, and an annual report covering "decommissioning security"; on 18 AUG the City Attorney described the draft development agreement as including financial assurance for decommissioning. <strong>No published document names an instrument, a surety or a secured amount</strong>, and none of it is in the 18 AUG text.<a class="fnref" href="#fs91"><sup>77</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs90"><sup>76</sup></a></li>
  <li><code>UPDATED</code> <strong>O.10, sewer discharge is not prohibited</strong>, only made subject to the city's pretreatment program under Chapter 13.24.<a class="fnref" href="#fs60"><sup>51</sup></a> The applicant told the Council on 18 AUG that the system <em>"would not discharge cooling water into the City's drainage or drinking water systems."</em><a class="fnref" href="#fs90"><sup>76</sup></a> The ordinance and that statement are not the same commitment, and only one of them is enforceable.</li>
  <li><code>UPDATED</code> No GHG provision, and no regulator in this process will supply one. DAQ quantifies CO2e in the Intent to Approve, 1,434,855 tons a year, but sets no CO2e condition; the only related limit is the fuel cap, 23,836 million cubic feet of gas per rolling 12 months, which is full nameplate at the permitted heat rate.<a class="fnref" href="#fs1"><sup>1</sup></a> A minor source needs no approval order for greenhouse gases at all under Utah Admin. Code R307-401-9.</li>
  <li><strong>The by-right mechanism, the one item on this list worth a question on 18 AUG.</strong> <code>UPDATED</code> With the struck text removed, Exhibit A contains <strong>no</strong> <em>"Conditional Use Permit"</em>, no <em>"conditional use"</em> and no <em>"Planning Commission may impose"</em>, against <strong>four</strong> uses of <em>"site plan application"</em>, at items 3 (twice), 6 and 9. All of those phrases are in the document, struck; the conditions clause in the applicant's July text (subsection L) is struck in full, and it is the only item in subsection O that was never given a number.<a class="fnref" href="#fs104"><sup>90</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs60"><sup>51</sup></a> <strong>The redline shows the removal was deliberate</strong>, striking conditional-use language from four separate clauses while leaving the heading's <em>"(see §15.3.08.060)"</em> cross-reference in place. <strong>§9 sets that out in full</strong>; both 21 SEP drafts resolve it.<a class="fnref" href="#fs115"><sup>101</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs116"><sup>102</sup></a> <code>UPDATED</code> The City Attorney confirmed the administrative reading on 18 AUG.<a class="fnref" href="#fs90"><sup>76</sup></a></li>
</ul>
<p><code>UPDATED</code> <strong>Of the six volunteered covenants, one is fully in the 18 AUG text</strong>: the noise limit (O.6). <strong>Four are partly there</strong>: electrical isolation, subject to a carve-out letting office, maintenance, custodial "and other similar portions" connect to the city system (O.5); diesel, excluded as prime power and ambiguous as standby, since the definition's fuel list and O.3 would exclude a diesel unit while O.12 preserves the codes governing emergency standby equipment; decommissioning, required without financial assurance (O.11); and water use, which becomes a permit condition at whatever estimate is approved rather than a fixed cap (O.9). Annual public reporting is missing. The applicant's 01 SEP presentation promises all six and more, but none of it is in an executed instrument, and no draft development agreement is on the 21 SEP agenda.<a class="fnref" href="#fs60"><sup>51</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs91"><sup>77</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs114"><sup>100</sup></a></p>
</section>

<section class="report-section wrap" id="the-best-question-on-the-agenda-exhibit-a-still-poin">
  <p class="section-label">9. The best question on the agenda</p>
  <h2>Exhibit A still points to the hearing chapter</h2>
<p class="lead"><code>UPDATED</code> <strong>Both 21 SEP drafts resolve this question.</strong> They add the data center uses to the I-1 zone's existing "Uses Subject to Conditions" list, which carries no hearing cross-reference, and never mention conditional use or §15.3.08.060, so nothing in either draft points to a hearing for approval (<a href="#the-21-sep-drafts">the 21 SEP drafts</a>).<a class="fnref" href="#fs13"><sup>11</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs115"><sup>101</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs116"><sup>102</sup></a> What follows describes the 18 AUG text. <strong>This was the strongest finding in the review, and no keyword search will surface it.</strong> It was found by following a cross-reference rather than searching for a phrase.</p>
  <h3 id="what-exhibit-a-contains-and-what-it-does-not">What Exhibit A contains, and what it does not</h3>
<p><strong>Exhibit A amends three sections</strong>: the definitions at §15.1.04.020, the I-1 use list at §15.3.16.120, and §15.3.24.090, where it adds subsection O, items 1 through 13. Read in full, two things are true at once.<a class="fnref" href="#fs60"><sup>51</sup></a> <code>UPDATED</code></p>
<p class="tbl-n">TABLE 12</p>
<div class="tw"><table>
<thead><tr><th><strong>In Exhibit A</strong></th><th><strong>Status</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td><em>"B. Uses Subject to Conditions <strong>(see §15.3.08.060)</strong>"</em></td><td>Present, in the I-1 use heading</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Any use of the phrase "conditional use permit"</strong></td><td>Absent</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Any grant of authority to the Planning Commission to impose conditions</strong></td><td>Absent</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>"site plan application" as the operating procedure, four times, at items 3, 6 and 9</strong></td><td>Present <code>UPDATED</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>O.7's cross-reference to <em>"the emission rates demonstrated under <strong>subsection C</strong>"</em></td><td>Dangling in the text analyzed on 17 AUG, since Exhibit A has no subsection C. <code>UPDATED</code> Fixed to "subsection 3" in the republished 18 AUG text</td></tr>
<tr><td>O.13's cross-reference to <em>"<strong>§15.3.16.120(C)</strong>"</em></td><td>Dangling in the text analyzed on 17 AUG, since Exhibit A renumbers that heading to B. <code>UPDATED</code> Fixed to §15.3.16.120(B) in the 18 AUG text</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p><strong>So the 18 AUG text routes these facilities through site plan review, contains no Planning Commission conditioning authority anywhere, and still carries a heading that points at the Conditional Use Permit section</strong>, where the public hearing and the five mandatory findings live.<a class="fnref" href="#fs13"><sup>11</sup></a> Whether that cross-reference imports those procedures or is inert decides whether any facility built under this text ever gets a hearing.</p>
  <h3 id="the-packet-contains-two-documents-that-describe-diff">The packet contains two documents that describe different ordinances</h3>
<p><strong>The justification statement traveled in the same 18 AUG packet the Council was to vote from</strong> (pp. 128 to 132), and on four points it did not describe the Exhibit A this report analyzed. <code>UPDATED</code> The republished 18 AUG Exhibit A took up two of them, the last two rows below:<a class="fnref" href="#fs60"><sup>51</sup></a></p>
<p class="tbl-n">TABLE 13</p>
<div class="tw"><table>
<thead><tr><th><strong>The justification statement says</strong></th><th><strong>Exhibit A says</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td><em>"Every project under this text still needs its own <strong>conditional use permit</strong>, its own <strong>public hearing</strong>, and its own site-specific conditions."</em></td><td>Neither phrase appears. Site plan review.</td></tr>
<tr><td><em>"The Planning Commission keeps <strong>full conditional use authority</strong> to impose site-specific conditions on each project."</em></td><td>No such authority appears anywhere in Exhibit A.</td></tr>
<tr><td><em>"Compliance is verified by <strong>sound testing after construction</strong> under the proposed text, not assumed from modeling alone."</em></td><td>In the text analyzed on 17 AUG, an acoustic analysis with the application and no post-construction testing. <code>UPDATED</code> Added to the republished Exhibit A on 18 AUG: O.6 now requires verification testing within 90 days of full operation.</td></tr>
<tr><td><em>"Every generator must meet an enforceable NOx limit of <strong>0.30 lb/MWh</strong>"</em>, with ultra-low at <strong>0.07</strong> and a graduated "enhanced review" tier</td><td>In the text analyzed on 17 AUG, a single tier at 0.5 lb/MWh. <code>UPDATED</code> Added to the republished Exhibit A on 18 AUG: the definition and O.3 now carry the 0.30 limit, the 0.07 Ultra-Low tier, the enclosure requirement, emissions monitoring and the Enhanced Review findings.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<div class="callout co-note">
  <div class="lbl">This is a documentary observation, not an accusation, and it should be made that way</div>
  <p>The 17 AUG ask was narrow: <em>"The justification statement in this packet describes a conditional use permit, a public hearing, post-construction sound testing, and a 0.30 lb/MWh enforceable limit. Exhibit A contains none of those. Which document is the Council adopting?"</em> <code>UPDATED</code> The 18 AUG text took up two: O.6 now requires verification testing within 90 days, and the definition carries the 0.30 limit with a 0.07 Ultra-Low tier. The question left on 18 AUG was the first one: the packet described a conditional use permit and a public hearing that the 18 AUG text did not contain.<a class="fnref" href="#fs60"><sup>51</sup></a></p>
</div>
<p><strong>One more feature of the text analyzed on 17 AUG.</strong> The definition of Ultra-Low Emission Generation Technology read <em>"includes, fuel cells, and linear generators meeting the foregoing standard."</em> There was no "without limitation" and no other technology named, so as written the list was arguably closed and would have excluded a future ultra-low technology without a further amendment. The 05 AUG Planning Commission packet shows the cause: staff struck ", without limitation," and ", recuperated microturbines," from that list (p. 63).<a class="fnref" href="#fs104"><sup>90</sup></a> <code>UPDATED</code> The 18 AUG definition of Controlled-Emission Generation Technology names no technologies, so this concern no longer applies.</p>
  <h3 id="the-cross-reference-itself">The cross-reference itself</h3>
<p><code>UPDATED</code> Exhibit A as republished is the amendment in tracked changes. Read with the deletions taken out, the heading is <strong>"B. Uses Subject to Conditions (see §15.3.08.060)."</strong> <strong>§15.3.08.060 is Title 15's <em>Conditional Use Permits</em> section. It makes the Planning Commission the land use authority, provides that it "shall hold at least one (1) public hearing," and requires five mandatory findings</strong> before approval.<a class="fnref" href="#fs13"><sup>11</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs60"><sup>51</sup></a></p>
<p><strong>Title 15 uses two established forms, and Exhibit A matches neither.</strong> A sweep of the code found <strong>17 headings</strong> reading <em>"Uses Subject to Conditional Use Permit (see §15.3.08.060)"</em>, where the cross-reference carries the hearing and findings machinery, and <strong>13 separate bare <em>"Uses Subject to Conditions"</em> headings, none of which carries it.</strong><a class="fnref" href="#fs13"><sup>11</sup></a> <code>UPDATED</code> The 17 AUG edition gave 17 and 16; a recount of the codified text confirms 17 and gives 13, and the point stands. <strong>Exhibit A creates a third form that exists nowhere else in the code: the "Conditions" label attached to the Conditional Use Permit cross-reference.</strong> That is the anomaly, and it is a residue of the heading it replaced.</p>
<p><strong>The redline settles what a string search could only suggest.</strong> The 05 AUG Planning Commission packet carries the amendment as tracked changes at pp. 62 to 65, and the republished 18 AUG Exhibit A carries the same result at pp. 122 to 123. <code>UPDATED</code> Rendered and read at the pixel level, because strikethrough does not survive text extraction, <strong>the drafters struck conditional-use language in four separate places and inserted "conditions" in three of them:</strong><a class="fnref" href="#fs104"><sup>90</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs60"><sup>51</sup></a></p>
<p class="tbl-n">TABLE 14</p>
<div class="tw"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Redline, 05 AUG Planning Commission packet pp. 63 to 64</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td><em>"Subsection C (Uses Subject to <del>Conditional Use Permit</del> <strong>Conditions</strong>)"</em></td><td>the subsection is retitled</td></tr>
<tr><td><em>"<del>C</del><strong>B</strong>. Uses Subject to <del>Conditional</del> <strong>Conditions</strong> <del>Use Permit</del> (see §15.3.08.060)"</em></td><td>heading changed, <strong>cross-reference left in place</strong></td></tr>
<tr><td><em>"in support of permitted and <del>conditionally permitted</del> <strong>uses subject to conditions</strong>"</em> (item 1, Purpose)</td><td>removed from the purpose clause</td></tr>
<tr><td><em>"applies to all Onsite Power Generation Facilities <del>approved as a conditional use</del> in the I-1 Zone"</em> (item 2, Applicability)</td><td><strong>"approved as a conditional use" struck</strong></td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p><strong>That is not drafting ambiguity. It is a deliberate, systematic removal of conditional-use status from every operative clause</strong>, and Exhibit A confirms the result.</p>
<p><strong>The surviving <em>"(see §15.3.08.060)"</em> is therefore best read as residue of the heading struck around it, not as deliberate incorporation.</strong> The by-right reading is the likely one, and it is what the 18 AUG text would have enacted. <strong>Deleting the stray cross-reference was a one-line amendment</strong> that would settle it, and both 21 SEP drafts drop it.<a class="fnref" href="#fs115"><sup>101</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs116"><sup>102</sup></a></p>
<p><strong>In the text analyzed on 17 AUG, two more dangling references pointed the same way.</strong> <strong>O.7</strong> required operating within emission rates <em>"demonstrated under subsection C"</em>, but the renumbered items 1 through 13 left no subsection C to point at, so the provision this report credited most hung on a reference to a subsection that did not exist. And <strong>O.13's</strong> two-site quota identified uses <em>"as identified in §15.3.16.120(C)"</em>, after the same ordinance renumbered that subsection to (B).<a class="fnref" href="#fs60"><sup>51</sup></a> <code>UPDATED</code> The republished 18 AUG text fixed both, to "subsection 3" and §15.3.16.120(B). The heading's <em>"(see §15.3.08.060)"</em> was the one left in the 18 AUG text.</p>
<p><strong>Why this was the right question and not a gotcha.</strong> There are two readings and they lead to opposite outcomes. <code>UPDATED</code> Utah courts try to give every word effect and avoid readings that make text superfluous (<em>Turner v. Staker &amp; Parson Cos.</em>, 2012 UT 30, ¶ 12), which favors incorporation: <em>"(see §15.3.08.060)"</em> imports the CUP section's procedures, every future applicant gets a Planning Commission hearing and findings, and the ordinance is far stronger than it appears. The competing reading treats the cross-reference as a drafting leftover, which is what the redline supports, and leaves the use purely administrative, with the entitlement running with the land and no future hearing.<a class="fnref" href="#fs13"><sup>11</sup></a></p>
<div class="callout co-insight">
  <div class="lbl">Ask:</div>
  <p>Exhibit A's heading reads "Uses Subject to Conditions" but cross-references §15.3.08.060, the Conditional Use Permit section, which requires a Planning Commission hearing and five findings.<a class="fnref" href="#fs13"><sup>11</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs60"><sup>51</sup></a> That cross-reference appears in no other "Uses Subject to Conditions" heading in Title 15. Which controls? And O.7 referenced "subsection C" while O.13 referenced §15.3.16.120(C), both of which this ordinance renumbered. Will these be corrected before adoption? <em>(The ask as put on 17 AUG.)</em></p>
</div>
<p><code>UPDATED</code> <strong>The republished text fixed both cross-references, and the City Attorney went further than either reading.</strong> On the record he said future site plans would be reviewed administratively by staff and the Development Review Committee, and that <em>"even if the use required a conditional use permit, the review would remain an administrative action handled by the Planning Commission rather than the City Council,"</em> with future reviews at public meetings but no additional public hearings.<a class="fnref" href="#fs90"><sup>76</sup></a> The minutes do not record him addressing the heading's cross-reference, which is still in the 18 AUG text. So the city's position on 18 AUG was that the outcome is the same either way, which is worth setting against §15.3.08.060(D)(2), still in the code today: <em>"The Commission shall hold at least one (1) public hearing on the application."</em><a class="fnref" href="#fs13"><sup>11</sup></a></p>
<p><strong>That is why the question was worth asking.</strong><a class="fnref" href="#fs90"><sup>76</sup></a> Under the 18 AUG text the entitlement runs with the land, subject only to expiration, revocation or decommissioning under O.11 and O.13. The ask rested on the enacting text itself, which is why it held regardless of what anyone intended. <code>UPDATED</code></p>
</section>

<section class="report-section wrap" id="the-air-permit-is-not-issued-and-the-comment-window-">
  <h2>10. The air permit is still not issued, and the comment window closed before any vote on adoption</h2>
<p class="lead"><strong>On 17 AUG, Volition's website said "Permit Status: Approved,"<a class="fnref" href="#fs4"><sup>4</sup></a> its August handout said "Utah DEQ issued the permit in 2026,"<a class="fnref" href="#fs5"><sup>61</sup></a> and its 04 AUG Council deck said a permit "has been approved,"<a class="fnref" href="#fs113"><sup>99</sup></a> as it told the Planning Commission on 05 AUG.<a class="fnref" href="#fs119"><sup>105</sup></a> All three were wrong</strong>, and the state's own document says so in terms. <code>UPDATED</code> The applicant corrected itself on 18 AUG, telling the Council <em>"we said it imprecisely and we're correcting it ourselves,"</em><a class="fnref" href="#fs103"><sup>89</sup></a> and its project page now reads "Intent to Approve," under public review through 05 SEP, with formal approval "expected mid-September."<a class="fnref" href="#fs4"><sup>4</sup></a> Its 01 SEP presentation calls the permit "pending final issuance."<a class="fnref" href="#fs91"><sup>77</sup></a> The comment period closed 05 SEP.<a class="fnref" href="#fs2"><sup>2</sup></a> A check of DAQ's list of issued Approval Orders on 22 SEP 2026 found <strong>none for this site</strong>.<a class="fnref" href="#fs110"><sup>96</sup></a></p>
