<div class="callout co-insight">
  <div class="lbl">Ask:</div>
  <p><code>UPDATED</code> The state's document is still only an Intent to Approve, issued 04 AUG 2026.<a class="fnref" href="#fs1"><sup>1</sup></a> Its comment period closed 05 SEP 2026, and no Approval Order had issued as of 22 SEP 2026; the applicant's own site still says formal approval is "expected mid-September."<a class="fnref" href="#fs44"><sup>28</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs110"><sup>96</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs4"><sup>4</sup></a> Why vote on the zoning before the permit that governs the facility exists?</p>
</div>
<p><strong>What the draft permit contains.</strong> <code>UPDATED</code> The Intent to Approve would authorize <strong>up to 1,210 solid-oxide fuel cell units at 325 kW each, 393.25 MW of combined nameplate</strong>, at a maximum heat rate of 7,127 Btu/kWh, using natural gas only. It is written to the equipment class, not to a manufacturer: it never names Bloom. SIC 4911 Electric Services, classified New Minor Source, with no applicable federal standards.<a class="fnref" href="#fs1"><sup>1</sup></a> Its project description also says the array would generate "325 MW of electricity"; its equipment line, 1,210 units at 325 kW, is 393.25 MW, and its NOx, CO, VOC and CO2e totals all compute at 393.25 MW.<a class="fnref" href="#fs1"><sup>1</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fd0"><sup>43</sup></a></p>
