<section class="report-section wrap" id="why-the-by-right-question-matters-far-more-here-than">
  <h2>11. Why the by-right question matters more here</h2>
<p class="lead">Utah enacted <strong>S.B. 132 (2025), "Electric Utility Amendments"</strong>, Laws of Utah 2025, ch. 318, effective <strong>07 MAY 2025</strong>, creating <strong>Utah Code Title 54, Chapter 26</strong>. It defines a new category at <strong>54-26-101(1)</strong>:<a class="fnref" href="#fs14"><sup>12</sup></a><a class="fnref" id="rs53" href="#fs53"><sup>44</sup></a></p>
<div class="callout co-note">
  <p><strong>"closed private generation system"</strong>, electric generating facilities and associated transmission infrastructure that <em>"(a) is not connected to and operates independently from the transmission system of a qualified electric utility, cooperative utility, municipal utility, or other utility; (b) serves one or more customers with a minimum cumulative electrical demand of <strong>100 megawatts</strong>; and (c) serves one or more large load customers through direct connection."</em><a class="fnref" href="#fs14"><sup>12</sup></a></p>
</div>
<p><code>UPDATED</code> <strong>That appears to describe this facility</strong>, at 2.5× the statutory threshold, and <strong>54-26-504(2)(a)</strong> provides that such systems "are exempt from commission oversight or regulation as a public utility."<a class="fnref" href="#fs53"><sup>44</sup></a> <strong>Whether the chapter reaches this site is unconfirmed.</strong> Its definitions turn on a "large load customer" in the service territory of a qualified electric utility, which in Utah means Rocky Mountain Power, and on a provider registered with the Public Service Commission; this parcel sits on Spanish Fork's own municipal power system.<a class="fnref" href="#fs53"><sup>44</sup></a> Ask the applicant whether it has registered under 54-26-501 and which statute it relies on. A conventional power plant of this size in Utah passes through several independent public-interest gates; if the chapter applies, this one passes through almost none:</p>
<p class="tbl-n">TABLE 17</p>
<div class="tw"><table>
<thead><tr><th><strong>Reviewer</strong></th><th><strong>Jurisdiction over this facility</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td><strong>Utah Public Service Commission</strong></td><td>Registration only, if Chapter 26 applies; exempt from regulation as a public utility, 54-26-504(2)(a) <code>UPDATED</code> <a class="fnref" href="#fs53"><sup>44</sup></a></td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>The electric utility</strong></td><td>None. No interconnection is requested. That is the point of the design <a class="fnref" href="#fs91"><sup>77</sup></a></td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Utah DAQ</strong></td><td>Air permit only. A minor-source approval order sets emission conditions; DAQ does not weigh siting, noise, heat, water, traffic, or land use <code>UPDATED</code> <a class="fnref" href="#fs1"><sup>1</sup></a></td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>FERC</strong></td><td>None. No transmission nexus</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Spanish Fork City land use</strong></td><td>The only discretionary public-interest review this facility will ever receive</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Title 54 Chapter 26 is a <strong>Public Utilities</strong> framework, silent on municipal zoning; it neither preempts local land-use authority nor supplies a substitute for it.<a class="fnref" href="#fs53"><sup>44</sup></a> So the entire public-interest review of a 393.25 MW industrial power plant reduces to one thing: the Spanish Fork zoning decision. The 18 AUG text of §15.3.24.090 converted that decision into a staff and Development Review Committee site-plan review at a public meeting, with no public hearing and, for Ultra-Low Emission equipment, no written findings.<a class="fnref" href="#fs60"><sup>51</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs90"><sup>76</sup></a> <code>UPDATED</code> Both 21 SEP drafts keep site plan review and add a development agreement for every facility and City Council approval of any Material Modification.<a class="fnref" href="#fs115"><sup>101</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs116"><sup>102</sup></a></p>
<p>This is not an argument that Volition is dangerous. It is an argument about <strong>where the only remaining valve is.</strong> The off-grid design that makes this project attractive is the same design that leaves the city as the only reviewer of where and whether it is built. Islanding is a benefit and a jurisdictional exit at once, and only one of those is in the deck. It also answers the rebuttal <em>"the state and DAQ will regulate this anyway"</em>: for a closed private generation system, the state has chosen not to regulate it as a utility, and DAQ's air permit does not reach siting.<a class="fnref" href="#fs14"><sup>12</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs53"><sup>44</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs1"><sup>1</sup></a> <code>UPDATED</code></p>
<p><strong>And the exemption is not incidental to the design. It is contingent on it.</strong> 54-26-101(1) defines a closed private generation system as one that <em>"is not connected to and operates independently from the transmission system of a qualified electric utility."</em><a class="fnref" href="#fs14"><sup>12</sup></a> <strong>Interconnection is physically available: a 345 kV hub sits under a mile north of the parcel.</strong><a class="fnref" href="#fs87"><sup>40</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fd0"><sup>43</sup></a> <code>UPDATED</code> <strong>Connecting would move the facility out of the closed category and into the chapter's "connected generation system" category</strong>, which is also exempt from regulation as a public utility but requires a connection contract approved by the Public Service Commission and interconnection under the transmission provider's FERC-approved tariff.<a class="fnref" href="#fs53"><sup>44</sup></a></p>
<div class="callout co-note">
  <div class="lbl">Both directions are fair</div>
  <p>The islanded design is why there is no rate impact, no socialized transmission cost, no substation build and no interconnection queue.<a class="fnref" href="#fs60"><sup>51</sup></a> It is also what leaves Spanish Fork land use as the only discretionary review. <code>UPDATED</code> If Chapter 26 applies, state law requires a closed system to stay wholly separate from utility facilities unless the Public Service Commission approves a connection contract (54-26-504(2)(b) and (c)), and the statute gives the city no say in that decision.<a class="fnref" href="#fs53"><sup>44</sup></a> <strong>In the 18 AUG text, O.5 is the city's own lever on the same question.</strong> Worth asking what happens to the zoning approval if a successor applies to interconnect.</p>
</div>
<p><strong>And the reporting backstop does not exist.</strong> <strong>S.B. 282 (2026)</strong> would have required an annual electricity- and water-consumption report from the large load customer to the host municipality; it failed and was never enrolled.<a class="fnref" id="rs58" href="#fs58"><sup>49</sup></a> <strong>H.B. 585 (2026)</strong>, which would have required data centers to disclose <em>"the percentage self-generated"</em> and <em>"the type of generation facility and fuel source,"</em> also failed.<a class="fnref" id="rs59" href="#fs59"><sup>50</sup></a> So no state law requires this facility to disclose its energy use or water; its air emissions are reported only through the state emission-inventory rules its draft permit invokes (condition I.7).<a class="fnref" href="#fs1"><sup>1</sup></a> The "annual public reporting" covenant Volition volunteered, which the 18 AUG text omitted, has no statutory backstop. If it is not in the ordinance or the development agreement, it does not exist. <code>UPDATED</code> The applicant's 01 SEP presentation promises an annual report by 31 MAR covering capacity, water, air, noise and incidents, and says Title 15 will require it, and the City Attorney's 18 AUG summary of Volition's draft development agreement includes annual public compliance and economic reports; the 18 AUG ordinance text does not.<a class="fnref" href="#fs91"><sup>77</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs90"><sup>76</sup></a> Both 21 SEP drafts now require an annual compliance report that the City makes public (subsection 21).<a class="fnref" href="#fs115"><sup>101</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs116"><sup>102</sup></a></p>
  <h3 id="is-by-right-an-outlier-in-utah-no-it-is-the-norm">Is by-right an outlier in Utah? No. It is the norm.</h3>
<p class="tbl-n">TABLE 18</p>
<div class="tw"><table>
<thead><tr><th><strong>Jurisdiction</strong></th><th><strong>Mechanism</strong></th><th><strong>MW cap</strong></th><th><strong>Data-center-specific standards</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td><strong>Eagle Mountain</strong> (hosts Meta)</td><td>Permitted (P), Industrial Zone, §17.40.020, administrative <a class="fnref" id="rs96" href="#fs96"><sup>82</sup></a></td><td>None</td><td>None. Ch. 17.75 Special Use Standards has zero data-center entries</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Bluffdale</strong> (hosts the NSA facility)</td><td>Permitted (P) in HC, RC, I-1, I-2, Table 11-35-2, administrative</td><td>None</td><td>None. No footnote on the use row</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Lehi</strong></td><td>By silence. No "data center" in the use table or definitions; falls under generic warehousing</td><td>None</td><td>None</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Saratoga Springs</strong></td><td>No provision; a use not listed <em>"is not permitted and is strictly prohibited,"</em> though an applicant may file a New or Unlisted Business Use application</td><td>N/A</td><td>N/A <a class="fnref" id="rs95" href="#fs95"><sup>81</sup></a></td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Utah County, unincorporated</strong></td><td>Affirmatively prohibited. §1.28: uses <em>"not expressly permitted… are hereby declared to be expressly prohibited"</em></td><td>N/A</td><td>N/A</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Box Elder County</strong></td><td>180-day moratorium, 10 JUN 2026 <a class="fnref" id="rs121" href="#fs121"><sup>107</sup></a></td><td>N/A</td><td>N/A</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Spanish Fork §15.3.24.090</strong> (proposed)</td><td>Use subject to conditions, staff and Development Review Committee site-plan review (administrative); both 21 SEP drafts add a development agreement</td><td>450 MW per generation facility, one data-center site citywide, in both 21 SEP drafts <code>UPDATED</code> <a class="fnref" href="#fs115"><sup>101</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs116"><sup>102</sup></a></td><td>In the 18 AUG text: 65/55 dBA, cooling method, emissions standard, electrical isolation, water condition, decommissioning, battery fire safety <code>UPDATED</code> <a class="fnref" href="#fs60"><sup>51</sup></a></td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>West Jordan</strong> (hosts Novva, Aligned)</td><td>Permitted (P), Title 13 Ch. 5 Art. F, M-P zone only; unlisted in M-1/M-2, and unlisted uses <em>"shall not be allowed"</em></td><td>None</td><td>None attached to the use</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p><code>UNVERIFIED</code> <em>The Bluffdale, Lehi, Utah County and West Jordan rows, and the Eagle Mountain row's code sections, rest on code reads not linked in this revision. Provo is not in the table: on 28 APR 2026 its council removed power generation as a permitted use for data centers and required non-evaporative cooling, and its March draft had barred islanded power plants.</em><a class="fnref" id="rs122" href="#fs122"><sup>108</sup></a></p>
<p><strong>Two conclusions follow, and they point in opposite directions.</strong></p>
<p><strong>1. On the mechanism, Spanish Fork is the Utah norm.</strong> Eagle Mountain and Bluffdale permit data centers administratively by a plain "P" in a use table; Lehi permits them by silence; <strong>Eagle Mountain moved <em>toward</em> this posture in 2024</strong>: on 03 SEP 2024 its Council voted 5 to 0 to repeal and replace its Industrial Zone chapter in an ordinance that, in staff's words, <em>"eliminates the conditional uses in each chapter."</em><a class="fnref" href="#fs96"><sup>82</sup></a> <code>UPDATED</code> (The 17 AUG edition gave an ordinance number and title the minutes do not show.) The criticism that Spanish Fork is doing something procedurally unusual does not survive.</p>
<p><strong>2. On the substance, Spanish Fork's 18 AUG text is the most regulated of the Utah jurisdictions in Table 18, by a wide margin.</strong> <code>UPDATED</code> Among them it is the only one with a megawatt cap, a citywide quota, a data-center-specific noise standard, a cooling-method restriction, an emissions requirement, an electrical-isolation mandate, a water condition, or a decommissioning provision. Eagle Mountain and Bluffdale host operating data centers under codes that impose none of those. Both 21 SEP drafts drop the 65 and 55 dBA limits and the stated cooling-method ban, keep the other six, and add a thermal-impact analysis, financial assurance and a public annual report.<a class="fnref" href="#fs115"><sup>101</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs116"><sup>102</sup></a></p>
<p><strong>That is a credit to the process that produced it:</strong> the applicant drafted most of these standards, staff and the Planning Commission added the floor, the cap and the quota, and the result carries more protection than the code of any Utah city that already hosts data centers, before the first one arrived.<a class="fnref" href="#fs104"><sup>90</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs60"><sup>51</sup></a> The criticism that remains is not that §15.3.24 is weak by comparison. It is that its specific gaps are real. <code>UPDATED</code> The 17 AUG edition listed four: no heat standard, no post-construction noise verification, no decommissioning security, no potential-to-emit cap. The 18 AUG text closed two, adding a 90-day sound verification test and a facility-wide NOx potential-to-emit cap. It left no heat standard and no decommissioning security; the applicant's 01 SEP presentation promises a heat study and a restoration estimate with City lien rights, and its draft development agreement adds financial assurance for decommissioning, none of it in the 18 AUG text.<a class="fnref" href="#fs60"><sup>51</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs91"><sup>77</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs90"><sup>76</sup></a></p>
<p><strong>One point of context.</strong> <code>UPDATED</code> Among jurisdictions that took up this question recently, Box Elder County adopted a moratorium<a class="fnref" href="#fs121"><sup>107</sup></a> and Provo's April 2026 ordinance removed power generation as a permitted use,<a class="fnref" href="#fs122"><sup>108</sup></a> while Eagle Mountain's use table, adopted in SEP 2024, predates the current wave.<a class="fnref" href="#fs96"><sup>82</sup></a> That distinction is real but weak, and should not lead. (The 17 AUG edition also named Saratoga Springs and Loudoun County; Table 18 shows Saratoga Springs has no provision at all, and the Loudoun item is not linked here.)</p>
</section>

<section class="report-section wrap" id="four-state-law-facts-nobody-in-the-spanish-fork-reco">
  <h2>12. Four state-law facts the Spanish Fork record has not addressed</h2>
<p class="lead">All four rest on statute or bill text read in full; three of the bills discussed failed. <code>UPDATED</code></p>
<p><strong>1. The fuel cells are already sales-tax exempt, and the rest may be too.</strong> <strong>Utah Code 59-12-104(80)</strong> exempts <em>"sales of a fuel cell as defined in Section 54-15-102,"</em> unconditionally and independent of anything about data centers.<a class="fnref" id="rs54" href="#fs54"><sup>45</sup></a> The Bloom array, plausibly the largest single equipment line in the project, is exempt on its own terms. Separately <strong>59-12-104(84)</strong> exempts equipment bought by a <em>"qualifying data center,"</em> which <strong>59-12-102(111)</strong> defines as a facility that <em>"(c) is a new operation constructed on or after July 1, 2016; (d) consists of one or more buildings that total 150,000 or more square feet."</em> The 1,054,000 sq ft clears (d);<a class="fnref" href="#fs64"><sup>55</sup></a> prong (c) is uncertain, since this is adaptive reuse of a building completed in <strong>1995</strong>.<a class="fnref" href="#fs19"><sup>16</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs20"><sup>17</sup></a> <code>UPDATED</code> (The 17 AUG edition cited subsection (109), renumbered (111) in the code effective 01 JUL 2026, and a 1998 date from the leasing listing.) A councilmember weighed an existing assessed value <em>"of approximately $50 million"</em> against <em>"$4 billion in equipment investment"</em> (04 AUG minutes, packet p. 70) with no offsetting entry, and sales and use tax on a $4B purchase is not a rounding error.<a class="fnref" href="#fs54"><sup>45</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs60"><sup>51</sup></a> The applicant's 18 AUG slides set Meta's state sales-tax exemption against Volition's "full tax paid from block one"; the fuel cells are exempt under 59-12-104(80) either way.<a class="fnref" href="#fs103"><sup>89</sup></a></p>
<div class="callout co-alert">
  <div class="lbl">Ask:</div>
  <p>Does this facility qualify under 59-12-104(84) given a building completed in 1995, and what sales and use tax does the city forgo?<a class="fnref" href="#fs54"><sup>45</sup></a> Do not assert a dollar figure. The fuel-cell exemption is certain; the data-center exemption's reach into a reused building is not.</p>
</div>
<p><strong>None of this contradicts the applicant's statement that Volition "had not requested tax incentives"</strong> (04 AUG minutes, packet p. 68).<a class="fnref" href="#fs60"><sup>51</sup></a> These exemptions are automatic, and <strong>H.B. 507 (2026)</strong> provides at 11-41-202(2)(e) that <em>"a sales and use tax exemption described in Section 59-12-104 does not constitute an incentive."</em><a class="fnref" href="#fs54"><sup>45</sup></a><a class="fnref" id="rs57" href="#fs57"><sup>48</sup></a></p>
<p><strong>2. Utah's only data-center disclosure law is keyed to water, and this design sits under it.</strong> <strong>H.B. 76 (2026)</strong> created <strong>Utah Code 73-5-8.3</strong>, requiring that <em>"before a land use authority… approves a land use application for a new large data center, the land use authority shall notify"</em> the Divisions of Water Rights and Water Quality and the water provider.<a class="fnref" id="rs56" href="#fs56"><sup>47</sup></a> <em>"Large data center"</em> is defined by <strong>annual withdrawal of 75 acre-feet or more.</strong> Volition's 250,000 gal/yr is <strong>0.77 acre-feet</strong>. Two orders of magnitude below the trigger.<a class="fnref" href="#fs60"><sup>51</sup></a> <code>UPDATED</code> That figure is the applicant's annual cooling and humidification make-up. Its own whole-site figure, 1 to 3 million gal/yr, is 3 to 9 acre-feet, still an order of magnitude below. <code>APPLICANT ONLY</code><a class="fnref" href="#fs91"><sup>77</sup></a></p>
<p>Read both ways: it is real evidence for the closed-loop design, <strong>and</strong> it means the state's only data-center notification regime does not reach this project. 73-5-8.3 expressly contemplates <em>"generation by the operator of the large data center of energy to power a specific facility,"</em> so the statute knows self-generation exists and still gates on water. Combined with the failure of S.B. 282 and H.B. 585: no Utah statute requires this facility to report its energy use, self-generation, or water.<a class="fnref" href="#fs56"><sup>47</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs58"><sup>49</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs59"><sup>50</sup></a></p>
<p><strong>3. An NDA with the city would now be a protected record.</strong> <strong>S.B. 318 (2026)</strong> would have barred cities, counties and state agencies from signing NDAs with prospective data centers and voided any signed after it took effect. It failed and was never enrolled. <strong>H.B. 507 did the opposite</strong>, amending <strong>63G-2-305(2)(b)</strong> to protect <em>"confidential economic development information… that takes the form of a nondisclosure agreement between a person and a governmental entity exploring an economic development opportunity."</em><a class="fnref" id="rs55" href="#fs55"><sup>46</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs57"><sup>48</sup></a> The Volition representative told Council conversations began <em>"around October or November"</em> (p. 65), roughly nine months before the application surfaced.<a class="fnref" href="#fs60"><sup>51</sup></a> <code>UPDATED</code> Any such NDA can now be classified as a protected record, though the city may still release it when the public interest in access is at least as strong (63G-2-201(5)(b)).</p>
<div class="callout co-insight">
  <div class="lbl">Ask plainly:</div>
  <p>Is there a nondisclosure agreement between the city and Volition or any affiliate? The existence of one is answerable without disclosing its contents.</p>
</div>
<p><strong>4. A mechanism now exists to divert the tax base being promised.</strong> H.B. 507 also enacted <strong>Title 63N, Chapter 3a, Part 4</strong>, creating "Regionally Significant Zones with Energy Implications." Under <strong>63N-3a-402(3)(b)</strong> a zone proposal may include a request to <em>"(i) capture up to 60% of the property tax increment… and (ii) divert up to 100% of personal property tax revenue"</em> generated within it.<a class="fnref" href="#fs57"><sup>48</sup></a> <strong>Most of the $40M/yr would be personal property tax on equipment.</strong><a class="fnref" href="#fs60"><sup>51</sup></a> From <strong>06 MAY 2027</strong> a municipality may generally <em>"not offer an incentive for a large load data center that is not located within a zone,"</em><a class="fnref" href="#fs57"><sup>48</sup></a> pushing large projects toward the mechanism. The zone and incentive rules reach only a "large load data center," which requires both qualifying-data-center status and a large load customer, and both are uncertain here (fact 1 and §11).<a class="fnref" href="#fs57"><sup>48</sup></a> <code>UPDATED</code> <strong>The applicant has already proposed the other route.</strong> Its 18 AUG slides describe a <em>"post-performance property-tax co-investment (Title 17C CRA)"</em> to be proposed to each taxing entity separately. A tax-increment rebate is an "incentive" under H.B. 507 (11-41-201), and an agreement or community reinvestment survey resolution adopted before 06 MAY 2027 is grandfathered (11-41-202(3)).<a class="fnref" href="#fs103"><sup>89</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs57"><sup>48</sup></a> So the fiscal case Council was shown treats the $40M as settled public revenue, while the applicant proposes rebating part of it and state law contains a pathway to route more of it elsewhere. Ask Nebo School District, Utah County and the city whether they have been approached, and for what share.</p>
</section>

<section class="report-section wrap" id="the-40m-year-does-not-mean-what-the-room-heard">
  <h2>13. The $40M/year does not mean what the room heard</h2>
<p class="lead"><strong>The year-one arithmetic is sound</strong>, and the city keeps about a tenth of it.<a class="fnref" href="#fs18"><sup>15</sup></a> The figure is gross, and gross is what an applicant quotes. None of the three corrections below challenges Volition's honesty. Its project page names <em>"the largest share flowing to Nebo School District"</em> and says the tax will decline <em>"as personal property depreciates."</em><a class="fnref" href="#fs4"><sup>4</sup></a> <code>APPLICANT ONLY</code> They are corrections to what the room is likely to have heard.</p>
  <h3 id="correction-1-75-of-it-is-not-the-city-s">Correction 1: 90% of it is not the city's</h3>
<p>Utah County Assessor, parcel <strong>67:228:0003</strong>, Tax Area 150.<a class="fnref" href="#fs12"><sup>10</sup></a> <strong>The combined approved rate for tax year 2026 is 0.009563</strong>:<a class="fnref" href="#fs18"><sup>15</sup></a></p>
