<tr><td><strong>Volition's offered community contribution alone</strong></td><td>&gt; $500,000 <a class="fnref" href="#fs60"><sup>51</sup></a></td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p><strong>The proposed contribution by itself is about ten times the city's share of the largest 2025 property tax bill found, and about twice what the city receives from the eleven taxpayers in Table 22 combined.</strong><a class="fnref" href="#fs66"><sup>57</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs80"><sup>73</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fd0"><sup>43</sup></a> <code>UPDATED</code> <strong>That is before a dollar of property tax.</strong></p>
<div class="callout co-insight">
  <div class="lbl"><code>COMMITTED, NOT EXECUTED</code></div>
  <p><code>UPDATED</code> <strong>Volition's 18 AUG slides say the $500,000 floor was "pledged on the Aug 4 record" and that "the instrument goes into the development agreement,"</strong> and its 01 SEP deck says the contribution is binding only once that agreement is executed. This report treats the pledge as made in good faith.<a class="fnref" href="#fs103"><sup>89</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs91"><sup>77</sup></a> The City Attorney's 18 AUG summary of Volition's draft agreement lists reporting, audit rights and decommissioning assurance, but not the contribution.<a class="fnref" href="#fs90"><sup>76</sup></a> <strong>No development agreement has been published or signed.</strong> The 18 AUG text did not require one.<a class="fnref" href="#fs60"><sup>51</sup></a> <code>UPDATED</code> Both 21 SEP drafts do (subsection 20), but neither names the contribution or a tax floor.<a class="fnref" href="#fs115"><sup>101</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs116"><sup>102</sup></a> <strong>The remaining question is form, not intent:</strong> ask that it be written as <strong>the revenue share the video describes rather than a flat figure</strong>, and ask for <strong>a floor under the tax projection</strong> in the same instrument.</p>
</div>
  <h3 id="correction-2-the-40m-is-a-floor-not-a-ceiling-and-it">Correction 2: the $40M likely excludes tenant servers, and it depreciates slowly</h3>
<p><code>UPDATED</code> <strong>The $4B figure most likely excludes the servers.</strong> Volition's own project page states the investment covers <em>"retrofit, fuel cells, electrical, fire, security, and engineering,"</em><a class="fnref" href="#fs4"><sup>4</sup></a> and its CEO told the Council on 18 AUG that the projections exclude customers' computing equipment.<a class="fnref" href="#fs90"><sup>76</sup></a> <code>APPLICANT ONLY</code> The 04 AUG minutes, though, record the $4B as <em>"power generation, electrical, and computing equipment,"</em> so the class split is unconfirmed.<a class="fnref" href="#fs60"><sup>51</sup></a> If the tenant installs the servers, that equipment is <strong>additional taxable value</strong>, in an amount nobody can state. The CEO also said revenue could be lower at a smaller build.<a class="fnref" href="#fs90"><sup>76</sup></a> <strong>Treat $40M/year as the applicant's full-build projection, with tenant equipment as possible upside.</strong></p>
