<tr><td><strong>1</strong></td><td>62% good, $23.7M</td><td>97% good, $37.1M</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>2</strong></td><td>46%, $17.6M</td><td>95%, $36.3M</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>3</strong></td><td>21%, $8.0M</td><td>92%, $35.2M</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>4</strong></td><td>9%, $3.4M</td><td>90%, $34.4M</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>5</strong></td><td>7%, $2.7M, the floor</td><td>87%, $33.3M <a class="fnref" href="#fs17"><sup>14</sup></a></td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>10</strong></td><td>7%, $2.7M</td><td>74%, $28.3M <a class="fnref" href="#fs17"><sup>14</sup></a></td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p><em>(Each column models the full $4B in that class, at the 2026 combined rate of 0.009563.)</em><a class="fnref" href="#fs17"><sup>14</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs18"><sup>15</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fd0"><sup>43</sup></a></p>
<p><code>UPDATED</code> <strong>On the applicant's description, Volition's $4B sits mostly on the durable side.</strong><a class="fnref" href="#fs4"><sup>4</sup></a> <code>APPLICANT ONLY</code> Fuel cells and electrical gear are generation equipment, and the retrofit is real property, reassessed at market rather than run down a percent-good schedule.<a class="fnref" id="rs74" href="#fs74"><sup>67</sup></a> <strong>If the compute is the tenant's, little of the $4B is Class 12</strong>, and generation equipment on the Class 27 schedule holds <strong>74% of its value at year ten</strong>.<a class="fnref" href="#fs17"><sup>14</sup></a></p>
<p><strong>Two things push the same direction.</strong> The Volition representative told Council the Bloom equipment could undergo <em>"a phased upgrade approximately seven or eight years after installation"</em> (packet p. 69).<a class="fnref" href="#fs60"><sup>51</sup></a> <code>APPLICANT ONLY</code> <code>UPDATED</code> Replacement equipment enters the roll as new, resetting the curve, and each tenant compute refresh re-enters it at 62% good.<a class="fnref" href="#fs17"><sup>14</sup></a></p>
<div class="callout co-note">
  <p><strong>The uncertainty runs both ways:</strong> nobody can size the tenant equipment, and nobody has confirmed the class split. Two things are worth asking for, neither adversarial. First, the tax pro forma: assumed class split, refresh cycle, and projected assessed value in years 1, 5 and 10. On 04 AUG a councilmember said <em>"the underlying figures needed to be clearly documented,"</em> speaking of the electricity, water, noise and air comparisons; the tax projection deserves the same.<a class="fnref" href="#fs60"><sup>51</sup></a> <code>UPDATED</code> Second, a revenue floor in the development agreement, which converts a projection into a commitment and costs the applicant nothing if the projection is sound.</p>
  <p>One caveat on the schedule itself. Class 27 is defined as <em>"plants designed to generate electrical power using turbogenerators."</em><a class="fnref" href="#fs17"><sup>14</sup></a> Solid-oxide fuel cells are not turbogenerators. If the Assessor classes them elsewhere the curve changes, which is another reason to ask for the pro forma rather than assume it.</p>
</div>
<p><strong>One thing not to do:</strong> do not net the sales-tax exemption against the property tax. <strong>59-12-104(84) is a sales and use tax exemption only.</strong> It does not touch property tax.<a class="fnref" href="#fs54"><sup>45</sup></a></p>
  <h3 id="correction-3-what-40m-year-means-against-what-these-">Correction 3: what $40M/year means against what these businesses pay</h3>
<p><strong>This is public record, and it should be quoted rather than computed.</strong> Utah County publishes the tax actually billed on every parcel, by year; <strong>2025 is the most recent complete year.</strong><a class="fnref" href="#fs66"><sup>57</sup></a> <code>UPDATED</code> <strong>Table 22 is eleven established commercial taxpayers, not a ranking.</strong> The largest 2025 parcel bill found in Spanish Fork is on the building Volition would occupy: $495,862 on one parcel, and $558,808 across its six parcels, close to the $561,000 the applicant's 18 AUG slides give for current taxes.<a class="fnref" href="#fs66"><sup>57</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs103"><sup>89</sup></a> At least 24 other parcels paid more than the smallest bill in the table. The table shows real-property bills only; these businesses also pay personal property tax on equipment.<a class="fnref" href="#fs17"><sup>14</sup></a></p>
