<p><strong>These eleven commercial taxpayers paid $2,483,765 between them in 2025. Volition's claimed $40M is sixteen times all of them combined</strong>, though the comparison sets mostly personal property against real-property bills, so it overstates the gap.<a class="fnref" href="#fs66"><sup>57</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fd0"><sup>43</sup></a> <code>UPDATED</code></p>
<p><strong>The hospital is not the benchmark it looks like:</strong> the city's most valuable property pays less than several smaller businesses, an effective 0.001734 against the 0.009632 to 0.009918 other parcels pay, reflecting the standard Utah charitable exemption.<a class="fnref" href="#fs62"><sup>53</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs66"><sup>57</sup></a><a class="fnref" id="rs67" href="#fs67"><sup>58</sup></a> <code>UPDATED</code></p>
  <h3 id="and-the-city-s-own-revenue-is-the-number-that-matter">The city's own revenue</h3>
<p><strong>Spanish Fork City's audited FY2025 statements report total property tax revenue of $5,885,340</strong>, against $5,741,117 the year before.<a class="fnref" href="#fs68"><sup>59</sup></a> That is the whole city: every home, every business, every parcel.</p>
<p class="tbl-n">TABLE 23</p>
