<tr><td>The city's own share, <strong>10.0%</strong> of the levy on $4B at the 2026 rate</td><td>~$3,830,000 <a class="fnref" href="#fd0"><sup>43</sup></a></td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p><strong>Two true statements, and the Council needs both.</strong> <strong>Volition's gross claim is 6.8× the city's entire property tax revenue</strong>, which is why the number sounds unreal in a Council chamber. But the realistic municipal benefit is roughly $3.8M a year, which would raise the city's own property tax revenue by about 65%, a large benefit, and smaller than the headline by a factor of ten. <strong>For scale, $3.8M is roughly 75&times; the city's share of the largest 2025 bill found, about $50,760, on the building Volition would occupy.</strong> <code>UPDATED</code> (The 17 AUG edition gave $4.0M, 68% and 120× against WestRock, computed on the rounded $40M.)<a class="fnref" href="#fs18"><sup>15</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs68"><sup>59</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fd0"><sup>43</sup></a> Setting $40M beside $5.9M compares a total against a share.</p>
<p><strong>Two rates are in play and are not interchangeable:</strong> the <strong>2026 approved rate of 0.009563</strong> for projections, and the <strong>0.009632 actually applied to 2025 bills</strong> for comparisons against real billed dollars.<a class="fnref" href="#fs18"><sup>15</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs66"><sup>57</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs80"><sup>73</sup></a> At the 2026 rate <strong>$4 billion produces $38.3M</strong> before depreciation, so <strong>$40M is close as a gross full-build figure.</strong><a class="fnref" href="#fd0"><sup>43</sup></a> <code>UPDATED</code></p>
  <h3 id="the-assessed-value-baseline-checks-out-the-acreage-i">The assessed-value baseline checks out, the acreage in testimony does not</h3>
<p>A councilmember put the property's existing assessed value at <em>"approximately $50 million"</em> (packet p. 70).<a class="fnref" href="#fs60"><sup>51</sup></a> That is <strong>confirmed: $50,898,400</strong> total assessed for 2026, owner of record WDC Industrial Utah Owner LP.<a class="fnref" href="#fs12"><sup>10</sup></a> <code>UPDATED</code> An <strong>$81,000,000</strong> figure in the parcel abstract is a trust deed securing a loan, not a sale price.<a class="fnref" href="#fs12"><sup>10</sup></a></p>
<p>The Volition representative told Council the building <em>"sat on approximately <strong>25 acres</strong>."</em><a class="fnref" href="#fs60"><sup>51</sup></a> <strong>The Assessor record shows 69.478641 acres</strong>, corroborated at 69.50 by computing the area of the state's parcel polygon.<a class="fnref" href="#fs12"><sup>10</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs62"><sup>53</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fd0"><sup>43</sup></a> <code>UPDATED</code> <strong>~23–25 acres is the single-story footprint, not the parcel</strong>, leaving roughly <strong>44 acres of open ground</strong> for the fuel-cell field, transformers, switchgear and cooling plant. It was the testimony that was wrong, not the plan.<a class="fnref" href="#fs64"><sup>55</sup></a> But note what else 44 acres means: <strong>the parcel could hold nearly the full 450 MW cap.</strong> At the density of the one sourced comparable, a 72.9 MW Bloom array on about 6.4 acres in Hilliard, Ohio, 450 MW would need about 40 acres, nearly all of the open ground.<a class="fnref" id="rs123" href="#fs123"><sup>109</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fd0"><sup>43</sup></a> <code>UPDATED</code> (The 17 AUG edition attributed that density to Bloom and said the parcel could hold the full entitlement twice over; no Bloom or applicant document states the density.)</p>
</section>

<section class="report-section wrap" id="how-utah-county-property-taxes-are-actually-calculat">
  <h2>14. How Utah County property taxes are calculated</h2>
<p class="lead"><strong>Every argument above about the $40M, and most of the neighborhood argument about property values, runs through one statutory mechanism.</strong> Getting it right changes what the Council should say about revenue.</p>
  <h3 id="the-chain-step-by-step">The chain, step by step</h3>
<p class="tbl-n">TABLE 24</p>
<div class="tw"><table>
<thead><tr><th><strong>When</strong></th><th><strong>What happens</strong></th><th><strong>Authority</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td><strong>01 JAN</strong></td><td>Valuation date. All tangible taxable property is assessed at fair market value as of 01 JAN, at a uniform and equal rate.</td><td>59-2-103(2) <a class="fnref" id="rs73" href="#fs73"><sup>66</sup></a></td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Every year</strong></td><td>The assessor annually updates values using a certified computer assisted mass appraisal system driven by a "systematic review of current market data." The mandatory annual all-parcel update applies to residential property.</td><td>59-2-303.1(2), (1)(a)(ii) <a class="fnref" href="#fs74"><sup>67</sup></a></td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Every year</strong></td><td>The State Tax Commission runs assessment studies measuring assessed against market value, then orders each county to factor its assessment rates. Factoring "may include an entire county, geographical areas within a county, and separate classes of properties."</td><td>59-2-704(1), (2) <a class="fnref" id="rs75" href="#fs75"><sup>68</sup></a></td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>At least once every 5 years</strong></td><td>The assessor completes a detailed review of the property characteristics of each parcel. This is the only step aimed at the individual parcel rather than at the model. <code>UPDATED</code></td><td>59-2-303.1(3)(a), (b) <a class="fnref" href="#fs74"><sup>67</sup></a></td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Value adjustment</strong></td><td>A qualifying primary residence receives a 45% reduction in value, on no more than one acre per residential unit.</td><td>59-2-103(3), (5) <a class="fnref" href="#fs73"><sup>66</sup></a></td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Before 01 JUN / by 13 JUN</strong></td><td>The assessor delivers aggregate valuations to the auditor, who transmits the certified tax rate to every taxing entity.</td><td>59-2-924(2), (3) <a class="fnref" id="rs76" href="#fs76"><sup>69</sup></a></td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>On or before 22 JUL</strong></td><td>Every owner is mailed the Notice of Property Valuation and Tax Changes, showing value, taxable value, any proposed increase, the hearing, and the appeal deadline.</td><td>59-2-919.1(1), (2) <a class="fnref" id="rs78" href="#fs78"><sup>71</sup></a></td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>15 SEP</strong>, or 45 days after that notice, whichever is later</td><td>Deadline to appeal to the county board of equalization.</td><td>59-2-1004(3)(a) <a class="fnref" id="rs79" href="#fs79"><sup>72</sup></a></td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>By 01 NOV</strong></td><td>The treasurer provides the tax notice, which must state that the property may be subject to a detailed review next year. <code>UPDATED</code></td><td>59-2-1317(2)(b)(iii), (7)(a) <a class="fnref" id="rs107" href="#fs107"><sup>93</sup></a></td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p><strong>That answers a common objection, that assessors cannot possibly drive every street in the county. They do not, and the statute never asks them to.</strong> Values move through mass appraisal and Commission-ordered factoring applied to geographic areas; a parcel-level review comes at least once every five years, and the statute does not require anyone to walk the property.<a class="fnref" href="#fs74"><sup>67</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs75"><sup>68</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs107"><sup>93</sup></a> <code>UPDATED</code></p>
  <h3 id="the-certified-tax-rate-is-the-part-almost-nobody-kno">The certified tax rate is the whole answer</h3>
<p><strong>Utah property tax is revenue-based, not rate-based.</strong><a class="fnref" href="#fs76"><sup>69</sup></a> The statute is blunt:</p>
<div class="callout co-note">
  <p><strong>"Certified tax rate" means a tax rate that will provide the same ad valorem property tax revenue for a taxing entity as was budgeted by that taxing entity for the prior year</strong>, calculated by dividing the ad valorem property tax revenue that a taxing entity budgeted for the prior year by the current year's adjusted aggregate taxable value.<a class="fnref" href="#fs76"><sup>69</sup></a></p>
</div>
<p><strong>Revenue is fixed first. The rate is whatever number produces it.</strong> A taxing entity may not levy above its certified rate, except during a truth-in-taxation exemption period, unless it advertises, mails notice, and holds a public hearing.<a class="fnref" id="rs77" href="#fs77"><sup>70</sup></a></p>
<p><strong>When values rise across a taxing entity, its certified rate comes down</strong>, and Spanish Fork's own records show it. <strong>Four large commercial parcels, four owners, five consecutive years, all in Tax Area 150</strong> carry an identical effective rate to six decimals in every year, because the rate belongs to the tax area and not the parcel; a fifth parcel in a different area returns a different rate, which confirms the reading:<a class="fnref" href="#fs80"><sup>73</sup></a></p>
<p class="tbl-n">TABLE 25</p>
<div class="tw"><table>
<thead><tr><th><strong>Tax year</strong></th><th><strong>Combined effective rate, Tax Area 150</strong></th><th><strong>Change</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td><strong>2021</strong></td><td>0.011230</td><td></td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>2022</strong></td><td>0.009881</td><td>−12.0%</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>2023</strong></td><td>0.009691</td><td>−1.9%</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>2024</strong></td><td>0.009702</td><td>+0.1%</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>2025</strong></td><td>0.009632</td><td>−0.7%</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p><strong>The rate fell 14.2% in four years. Nobody voted for a tax cut.</strong> That is Truth in Taxation operating as written.<a class="fnref" href="#fs80"><sup>73</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs76"><sup>69</sup></a></p>
  <h3 id="so-why-did-an-announcement-next-door-not-raise-your-">So why did an announcement next door not raise your taxes</h3>
<p><strong>Because rising value alone does not raise your bill.</strong> What raises your bill is your value rising faster than the average inside your taxing entity, or the entity voting an increase after a noticed hearing.<a class="fnref" href="#fs77"><sup>70</sup></a> The same parcels show it:<a class="fnref" href="#fs80"><sup>73</sup></a></p>
<p class="tbl-n">TABLE 26</p>
<div class="tw"><table>
<thead><tr><th><strong>Parcel</strong></th><th><strong>Taxable value, 2021 to 2025</strong></th><th><strong>Actual tax billed, 2021 to 2025</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td><strong>Costco Wholesale</strong></td><td>+44.7%</td><td>+24.1%</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Longview Fibre / WestRock</strong></td><td>+57.3%</td><td>+34.9%</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Hydro Extrusion USA</strong></td><td>+37.4%</td><td>+17.9%</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Nature's Sunshine Products</strong></td><td>+34.8%</td><td>+15.6%</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p><strong>Each gained between a third and nearly three fifths in taxable value, and none saw its bill rise anywhere near that much.</strong> The gap is the certified rate falling underneath them.<a class="fnref" href="#fs80"><sup>73</sup></a></p>
<p><strong>Three consequences to carry to the podium:</strong> a new landmark, temple, park, school or data center does not raise your taxes by existing, only by making <em>your</em> property appreciate faster than the average inside your taxing entity; an announcement is not evidence of value, since the annual update runs on current market data and factoring on assessment studies, so a change appears only once transactions demonstrate it;<a class="fnref" href="#fs74"><sup>67</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs75"><sup>68</sup></a> and the common error is reverse attribution: people see the bill move, look at what changed nearby, and connect the two, when the bill moved because of a countywide ratio study and a divisor.</p>
  <h3 id="the-finding-that-matters-for-this-amendment">The finding that matters for this amendment</h3>
<p><strong>New growth is deliberately excluded from the certified rate denominator.</strong> The statute subtracts eligible new growth from the value the prior year's revenue is divided by, and "locally assessed new growth" expressly excludes any change in value "as a result of factoring in accordance with Section 59-2-704, reappraisal, or another adjustment."<a class="fnref" href="#fs76"><sup>69</sup></a> It captures new construction, not market drift. Two consequences follow, and the room needs both:</p>
<div class="callout co-warning">
  <div class="lbl">TRUE, and it is the strongest fiscal argument the applicant has</div>
  <p>Revenue from a new facility is additive in the year it arrives. It sits on top of the prior year's budgeted revenue, requires no Truth in Taxation hearing, and does not come out of any existing taxpayer's pocket.<a class="fnref" href="#fs76"><sup>69</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs77"><sup>70</sup></a></p>
  <p><code>UPDATED</code> <strong>After that year the formula runs the other way.</strong> The certified rate carries last year's revenue forward, so as the facility's equipment depreciates, the same budget is spread over a smaller base and, all else equal, the rate rises for everyone else without a hearing, because the entity is still at its certified rate.<a class="fnref" href="#fs76"><sup>69</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs77"><sup>70</sup></a> Over the depreciation life, other taxpayers absorb part of what the facility contributed in its first year.</p>
  <p><strong>FALSE, and it should stop being said.</strong> The formula does not lower existing owners' taxes when a data center arrives. The certified rate is computed before new growth is added back, which is the point of excluding it. Nothing in Section 59-2-924 reduces an existing owner's bill because a new taxpayer arrived.<a class="fnref" href="#fs76"><sup>69</sup></a></p>
</div>
<p><strong>One provision worth an ask.</strong> <code>UPDATED</code> Section 59-2-924 already keeps one class of high-value equipment out of the certified rate on both sides of the fraction, the revenue and the value: semiconductor manufacturing equipment.<a class="fnref" href="#fs76"><sup>69</sup></a> That is not a tax exemption. The equipment is still assessed and still billed; it is left out of the formula that sets everyone's rate, which is the closest thing in the statute to an answer for fast-depreciating equipment.</p>
<div class="callout co-insight">
  <div class="lbl">Ask:</div>
  <p>Does the development agreement address what happens to the city's revenue expectation, and to other taxpayers' rates, as the facility's equipment depreciates, or if the Legislature later treats data center equipment the way this section already treats semiconductor manufacturing equipment?</p>
</div>
</section>

