  <h3 id="the-carbon-numbers-these-claims-are-measured-against">The carbon numbers these claims are measured against</h3>
<p><strong>Bloom's 04 FEB 2026 datasheet for the ES 6.5 publishes 679–833 lb CO2/MWh</strong>, and that printed figure reconciles to 0.1% with the datasheet's own heat rate through EPA's standard natural-gas carbon factor.<a class="fnref" href="#fs3"><sup>3</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs31"><sup>25</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fd0"><sup>43</sup></a> Utah DAQ's Intent to Approve puts the facility's potential CO2e at 1,434,855 short tons per year, 1,301,679 metric tons (DAQE-IN163550001-26, 04 AUG 2026).<a class="fnref" href="#fs1"><sup>1</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fd0"><sup>43</sup></a> <strong>At 75% of the proposed nameplate the figure is ~796,000–977,000 metric tons/yr.</strong><a class="fnref" href="#fd0"><sup>43</sup></a> <code>UPDATED</code> The 75% is the applicant's own average utilization, given for a 250 MW maximum (04 AUG minutes, packet p. 69); applying it to 393.25 MW is this report's assumption.<a class="fnref" href="#fs60"><sup>51</sup></a> Methane in a solid-oxide fuel cell yields CO2 as its primary carbon product: suppressing combustion eliminates thermal NOx, not carbon.<a class="fnref" href="#fs1"><sup>1</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fd0"><sup>43</sup></a> Upstream methane leakage adds roughly 120,000–197,000 metric tons CO2e (100-year basis) at the proposed 393.25 MW and 75% load.<a class="fnref" href="#fd0"><sup>43</sup></a> <code>UPDATED</code> (The 17 AUG edition gave 76,000–125,000, computed at 250 MW.)</p>
