<p><strong>The estimate is more likely low than high.</strong> In the one field record found, reported by NBC Bay Area's Investigative Unit, <strong>Delaware installations averaged 823 lb CO2/MWh over 24 months against Bloom's advertised 773</strong> (25 JUN 2015), and a separate installation, AC Transit, held the advertised rate only for the first three of 22 months.<a class="fnref" id="rs61" href="#fs61"><sup>52</sup></a> <code>UPDATED</code> That is a decade old and predates this platform, and 823 sits inside the ES 6.5's published 679 to 833 band, so it does not show the datasheet understates.<a class="fnref" href="#fs3"><sup>3</sup></a> It shows field averages landing near the top of the band rather than the middle, which makes the midpoint used above the optimistic end. <strong>Even at 823 lb/MWh Bloom still beats the Utah grid</strong>, which EIA puts at <strong>0.601 metric tons CO2/MWh for 2024, about 1,325 lb/MWh</strong>.<a class="fnref" href="#fs43"><sup>27</sup></a> The criticism is the gap between marketing and measurement, not the absolute number.</p>
  <h3 id="co2-the-same-amount-as-three-or-four-diesel-trucks">1. CO2 equal to "three to four diesel trucks"</h3>
<p>The answer depends on scope, and <strong>the claim states none.</strong> On <strong>total facility CO2</strong> the permit's 1,301,679 metric t/yr against 3–4 trucks at 158 t each (an assumed 100,000 miles a year at 6.46 mpg, and EPA's 10.21 kg CO2 per gallon of diesel) <strong>fails by roughly 2,060–2,750×</strong>.<a class="fnref" href="#fs1"><sup>1</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs31"><sup>25</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fd0"><sup>43</sup></a> On <strong>criteria pollutants alone</strong>, the basis a Volition representative described to the Planning Commission (minutes p. 38),<a class="fnref" href="#fs119"><sup>105</sup></a> the comparison can come out small, but that basis leaves out the permit's own CO2e row. <code>UPDATED</code> On 18 AUG the applicant conceded the point: <em>"we understated it, and we've since corrected it on the record: about 1.43 million tons a year at full build."</em><a class="fnref" href="#fs103"><sup>89</sup></a> (The 17 AUG edition used 11.08 kg per gallon, 171.5 t per truck and 1,900–2,500×, and converted criteria pollutants to CO2-equivalent with indirect warming values that no single authority publishes; that reading is withdrawn.)</p>
<div class="callout co-warning">
  <div class="lbl">The basis was the finding, and the applicant has since corrected it</div>
  <p><code>UPDATED</code> The permit's emissions table puts the criteria pollutants and VOC at about 45 tons a year by mass, and its conditions set rates for NOx, CO and VOC. The same table prints 1,434,855 short tons a year of CO2e, and no condition limits it; only the gas cap, 23,836 million cubic feet per rolling 12 months, bounds it.<a class="fnref" href="#fs1"><sup>1</sup></a> A comparison that quietly drops that row is not a lie; it is an unstated basis that swings the answer by more than three orders of magnitude.<a class="fnref" href="#fd0"><sup>43</sup></a> The applicant's 18 AUG slides now say <em>"we do not claim zero carbon."</em><a class="fnref" href="#fs103"><sup>89</sup></a></p>
</div>
<p><strong>One carbon claim holds up, with one qualification.</strong> The 04 AUG deck's <em>"lowest-available carbon at this scale"</em> is right against on-site engines and against the grid, and it is the framing Volition should have used.<a class="fnref" href="#fs113"><sup>99</sup></a> <code>UPDATED</code> Against a modern combined cycle it is close: Lake Side's own filings work out to about 0.373 to 0.381 t/MWh on gross generation, so the fuel cells lead by 8 to 10% at their midpoint and not at all at end of life.<a class="fnref" href="#fs92"><sup>78</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs31"><sup>25</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fd0"><sup>43</sup></a></p>
