<p><code>UNVERIFIED</code> The prior-identity and employee rows, and the website row's 12 AUG reading, are not linked to a public source in this revision.</p>
<p><code>UPDATED</code> The applicant told the Planning Commission the project was <em>"fully capitalized,"</em><a class="fnref" href="#fs119"><sup>105</sup></a> but <strong>no SEC filing shows the capital behind a ~$4B claim.</strong><a class="fnref" href="#fs60"><sup>51</sup></a> The gas lateral alone is about $14M by the applicant's own account. <code>APPLICANT ONLY</code><a class="fnref" href="#fs91"><sup>77</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs98"><sup>84</sup></a> (The 17 AUG edition gave a capital ratio of about 1:1,430 on a $2.8M figure that does not hold.)</p>
<p>Two claims need qualifying. <strong>"500+ MW of team experience"</strong> and the Novva overlap are on the record: the 05 AUG Planning Commission minutes have the Volition representative saying the development team <em>"has collectively built more than 500 megawatts of data centers, including the Novva facility."</em><a class="fnref" href="#fs119"><sup>105</sup></a> But the only company material this review found gives the figure as <em>"over 500MW in new development in the works"</em>, a forward pipeline, not a track record, and no named Novva executive matches the team: <strong>unverified, not disproven.</strong> <code>UNVERIFIED</code> The pipeline claims thin out: the representative named potential sites in San Jose and Austin,<a class="fnref" href="#fs119"><sup>105</sup></a> and nothing was found for either; "Stockman Flats" is a real place: Spanish Fork created three Public Infrastructure Districts there at owner petition on 21 APR 2026, but the record carries no MW figure, no data center reference and no Volition mention.<a class="fnref" id="rs129" href="#fs129"><sup>115</sup></a></p>
<p><strong>The counter-case.</strong> Bloom Energy is the named supplier in the city record: Volition would buy the fuel cells under a long-term Bloom service agreement, and says it has secured inventory.<a class="fnref" href="#fs60"><sup>51</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs119"><sup>105</sup></a> Volition's website has offered, since at least JUL 2023, solar, batteries and other equipment installed on customers' properties at no capital cost to them, and by FEB 2025 fully funded microgrids that sell the energy at fixed rates for 20 to 30 years. That business is <strong>adjacent to the hardest part</strong>; the data center half is what is new.<a class="fnref" href="#fs125"><sup>111</sup></a> Adaptive reuse reduces execution risk, the build is phased,<a class="fnref" href="#fs91"><sup>77</sup></a> and the company volunteered enforceability: its own July text proposed conditional use review with a public hearing for every project, which staff's 05 AUG redline replaced with administrative review.<a class="fnref" href="#fs104"><sup>90</sup></a> <code>UPDATED</code></p>
<p><strong>The problem is not bad faith. It is capitalization and track record</strong>, against a permanent entitlement.</p>
</section>

<section class="report-section wrap" id="the-tenant-chain">
  <h2>19. The tenant chain</h2>
<p class="lead"><code> Spanish Fork City → Volition Industries, no operating facility, no capital raise in any SEC filing, no SPV named → Nscale (alleged), $14.6B valuation, $140.6M revenue and a $1.02B net loss in H1 2026, $103B contracted backlog → an undisclosed end customer </code><a class="fnref" id="rs130" href="#fs130"><sup>116</sup></a><a class="fnref" id="rs131" href="#fs131"><sup>117</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs119"><sup>105</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs98"><sup>84</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs60"><sup>51</sup></a></p>
<p><code>UPDATED</code> <strong>Nscale's public materials do not mention Spanish Fork or Volition</strong>: not its site list, newsroom, trade press or 18 SEP 2026 IPO filing, whose subsidiary list names Utah only in "Nscale GPU Holdings Utah, LLC" and "Nscale GPU Utah, LLC," with no site.<a class="fnref" href="#fs130"><sup>116</sup></a> Pre-signing NDAs are standard, so this is not evidence of fabrication. It means the city has no independent corroboration of the tenant on a project whose entire economic case depends on one.</p>
<p><code>UPDATED</code> Nscale's two largest contracts are now with <strong>Microsoft and Anthropic</strong>, up to about $44 billion each, and OpenAI withdrew from its Stargate UK and Stargate Norway partnerships with Nscale in APR 2026.<a class="fnref" href="#fs130"><sup>116</sup></a> The 05 AUG Planning Commission minutes record the Volition representative saying neither Anthropic nor OpenAI was currently planning for the facility, although discussions had occurred.<a class="fnref" href="#fs119"><sup>105</sup></a> (The 17 AUG edition recorded Microsoft and OpenAI, which no posted record carries.) That does not identify this site's sub-tenant. The closest structural precedent is public: Ionic Digital Inc. → Nscale → Microsoft at Ward County, Texas, a 126-month triple-net lease for 234 MW, where Ionic Digital's SEC filing states <em>"We currently have only one customer"</em> and warns explicitly about Nscale nonperformance.<a class="fnref" id="rs132" href="#fs132"><sup>118</sup></a><a class="fnref" id="rs133" href="#fs133"><sup>119</sup></a> Same chain shape, with the difference that Ionic is public and discloses the risk.</p>
<p><strong>The commitment to bar <em>"U.S. government surveillance work or private surveillance companies"</em> is almost certainly sincere and almost certainly unenforceable.</strong><a class="fnref" href="#fs60"><sup>51</sup></a> The city has no privity with the sub-tenant's sub-tenant, GPU workload content is not externally verifiable, and no public record of a municipality enforcing such a restriction was found. Better to ask for an audit right than accept a promise.</p>
</section>

<section class="report-section wrap" id="sound-and-heat-the-two-things-that-reach-a-neighbor">
  <h2>20. Sound and heat</h2>
<p class="lead"><strong>The nearest residential lot line is about 1,300 ft from the property boundary</strong>, to the west; the nearest house itself is roughly 1,500 to 1,800 ft away.<a class="fnref" href="#fs62"><sup>53</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fd0"><sup>43</sup></a> <code>UPDATED</code> (The 17 AUG edition gave 1,298 ft without stating that it runs property line to property line.) The nearest homes are about a third of a mile away, not a mile. And because the amendment applies citywide, the receptor that matters is the nearest home to whichever I-1 parcel an applicant selects.</p>
