<p><strong>Heat</strong> is not perceptible at a mile, is not modeled here at the nearest homes, and has no numeric limit in any draft; sound level at the nearest homes depends on where the equipment sits on the parcel; sound character is the real exposure, which the 18 AUG text did not address and both 21 SEP drafts now require the acoustic analysis to model.<a class="fnref" href="#fd0"><sup>43</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs115"><sup>101</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs116"><sup>102</sup></a> <code>UPDATED</code></p>
  <h3 id="heat">Heat</h3>
<p><strong>605 to 742 MW-thermal</strong> at the proposed 393.25 MW is a real quantity that disperses fast: on-site temperature rise falls from <strong>4.3 to 5.3 °F at 5 m/s to 1.6 to 2.0 °F at 30 mph</strong>, and at a mile it is negligible.<a class="fnref" href="#fs1"><sup>1</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fd0"><sup>43</sup></a> <code>UPDATED</code> The 17 AUG edition printed 3.0 °F and 1.1 °F, which came from a smaller 417 MW-thermal illustration rather than the proposed scale. The applicant's own figures moved on 18 AUG to up to about nine degrees on site under certain conditions and under one degree before about half a mile, replacing the 5 to 6 degrees next to the property and under one degree at about a quarter mile it gave the Planning Commission on 05 AUG.<a class="fnref" href="#fs90"><sup>76</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs119"><sup>105</sup></a> Nobody will feel this at a mile. This report does not compute the rise at the nearest houses, about a third of a mile away, and the applicant's own figure, under one degree only beyond about half a mile, does not rule out a rise of a degree or more there. The applicant also gives total heat at full load as about 553 MW-thermal <code>APPLICANT ONLY</code>; at the proposed 393.25 MW that would take about 71% efficiency, above Bloom's published 65%, so it appears to describe a smaller load, and the range here uses Bloom's published efficiencies.<a class="fnref" href="#fs103"><sup>89</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs3"><sup>3</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fd0"><sup>43</sup></a> In the 18 AUG text the gap is regulatory, not physical: its §15.3.24.090 contains no thermal standard at all, no modeling requirement and no measurement.<a class="fnref" href="#fs60"><sup>51</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fd0"><sup>43</sup></a> The applicant's 01 SEP presentation promises a third-party, site-specific heat study before operation.<a class="fnref" href="#fs91"><sup>77</sup></a> <code>UPDATED</code> Both 21 SEP drafts now require a thermal-impact analysis before site plan approval, with conditions that <em>"shall include ongoing monitoring at the facility and locations off-site,"</em> but neither sets a numeric limit.<a class="fnref" href="#fs115"><sup>101</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs116"><sup>102</sup></a></p>
<div class="callout co-insight">
  <div class="lbl">Ask:</div>
  <p>Both 21 SEP drafts now require the heat study and off-site monitoring. Why does neither set a numeric thermal limit? <code>UPDATED</code></p>
</div>
  <h3 id="sound-level">Sound level</h3>
<p><code>UPDATED</code> <strong>At a receptor one mile away, 1,210 modules give 15 to 40 dBA against a 30 to 40 dBA rural night ambient. The nearest houses are much closer, 1,500 to 1,800 ft from the property line, and there the same model gives roughly 25 to 51 dBA if the fuel cells sit on the side of the parcel nearest the homes, and about 19 to 45 dBA if they sit on the far side</strong>.<a class="fnref" href="#fd0"><sup>43</sup></a> Where the equipment will sit on the parcel is not known. <strong>That ambient floor is not quiet in practice.</strong> <code>SELF-ATTESTED</code> A rail crossing sits directly across US-6 from the nearest residential receptors and its horn sounds through the day and night, which is the loudest recurring sound most of these homes already live with. <strong>The 25 dB spread is what the public record supports</strong>, not either endpoint, and it covers everything from inaudible to clearly present. Treat every figure as a lower bound: inverting a <em>"&lt;65 dBA @ 10 ft"</em> spec as a point source is wrong for a cabinet 29 feet long, and finite-line and ISO 3744 treatments give <strong>84.5 and 90.6 dBA</strong> sound power against the 82.7 used.<a class="fnref" href="#fs3"><sup>3</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fd0"><sup>43</sup></a> The 18 AUG text's O.6 set 55 dBA at the residential zone boundary at all hours; both 21 SEP drafts drop it and apply Chapter 9.32, whose nighttime limit at residential boundaries the 18 AUG text called equivalent.<a class="fnref" href="#fs60"><sup>51</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs115"><sup>101</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs116"><sup>102</sup></a></p>
<p><strong>The cooling plant, not the fuel cells, is the likely dominant source.</strong><a class="fnref" href="#fd0"><sup>43</sup></a> <code>UPDATED</code> Most data centers are loudest when they occasionally run generators; at Vantage in Loudoun County the constant noise comes from gas turbines.<a class="fnref" id="rs99" href="#fs99"><sup>85</sup></a> The 18 AUG text bars diesel prime power and both 21 SEP drafts bar backup diesel as well, which removes the loudest source,<a class="fnref" href="#fs60"><sup>51</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs115"><sup>101</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs116"><sup>102</sup></a> but cooling runs around the clock. Residents of Sturtevant, Wisconsin filed a class action in 2026 over the Mount Pleasant data center, calling its noise <em>"not only excessive, but consistent and pervasive"</em> and blaming HVAC and diesel generators.<a class="fnref" id="rs134" href="#fs134"><sup>120</sup></a></p>
<p><strong>In the 18 AUG text, the binding number is stricter than the criticism suggests.</strong> Its O.6 sets 65 dBA at the property line and 55 dBA at any residential zone boundary, at all hours, against the existing I-1 limits of <strong>80 dBA by day and 65 dBA at night</strong>.<a class="fnref" href="#fs60"><sup>51</sup></a> <code>UPDATED</code> By day that is 15 dB tighter, about a two-thirds cut in perceived loudness; at night it matches today's limit, so the city's nighttime standard applies around the clock. <strong>The number is good, and the enforcement is better than this report first stated.</strong> O.6 binds <em>"at any hour"</em>, expressly covers <strong>cooling equipment</strong>, adopts the <strong>Chapter 9.32 measurement procedures</strong>, and adds that <em>"compliance with this subsection does not excuse compliance with Chapter 9.32."</em><a class="fnref" href="#fs60"><sup>51</sup></a> The limit is continuing and independently enforceable, not a design target. <code>UPDATED</code> <strong>The 18 AUG text also added what the 17 AUG edition found missing</strong>: a sound verification test within 90 days of full commercial operation, and it lets the Zoning Administrator require follow-up testing on a substantiated complaint.<a class="fnref" href="#fs60"><sup>51</sup></a> <code>UPDATED</code> <strong>Both 21 SEP drafts remove the 65 and 55 dBA limits</strong> and apply Chapter 9.32's zone limits, with the most restrictive limit at a zone boundary. They require the acoustic analysis to model tonal and low-frequency sound and predict levels at the nearest existing residences, with testing after each phase to show those levels are not exceeded; see <a href="#the-21-sep-drafts">the 21 SEP drafts</a>.<a class="fnref" href="#fs115"><sup>101</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs116"><sup>102</sup></a></p>
  <h3 id="field-observations-at-an-operating-comparable-14-aug">Field observations at an operating comparable, 14 AUG 2026 <code>UNCALIBRATED</code> <code>SELF-ATTESTED</code></h3>
<p><strong>On 14 AUG 2026, between roughly 16:00 and 18:00, the author visited the Novva Data Center in West Jordan</strong>, interviewed six people and took uncalibrated phone-based sound readings.<a class="fnref" href="#ff1"><sup>60</sup></a></p>
<p><strong>Method:</strong> perimeter roads driven <strong>twice, clockwise, at a constant 18 MPH on cruise control</strong>, only the passenger window open, microphone pointed at the property. Constant speed holds the car's own noise steady, which is what makes the <em>relative</em> comparisons meaningful even though the absolute level is not. <strong>Readings approximate property-line distance</strong>, though the road-to-fence setback was not recorded.<a class="fnref" href="#ff1"><sup>60</sup></a></p>
<p><strong>What was found.</strong> Readings ran <strong>approximately 65 dB</strong>, but wind, truck traffic, construction and neighboring commercial activity were audibly louder than any data center equipment at every point. All six interviewees reported no noise issues, all six volunteered that neighboring activity is louder than the site, and all six said they had never known the diesel generators to run and knew of no outage.<a class="fnref" href="#ff1"><sup>60</sup></a> The site's permit allows generator testing only between 07:00 and 19:00.<a class="fnref" href="#fs47"><sup>31</sup></a> <code>UPDATED</code></p>
<p>The six were <strong>two residents living directly across the street</strong>, two employees of the neighboring construction supplier, a security guard at that business, and Novva's own guard. Five of the six have no interest in defending the site, and the two most probative are the residents, the closest available analogue to the receptor this ordinance protects.<a class="fnref" href="#ff1"><sup>60</sup></a></p>
<div class="callout co-note">
  <div class="lbl">The visit was unannounced</div>
  <p>It was taken from public roads, with no contact with the operator, so nothing could be throttled or shut down to present a quieter site. A commissioned study is far more precise and is taken with notice; this was far less precise and taken without any. The failure modes are opposite, which is why both belong in the record.</p>
  <p><strong>A cheap condition, if the Council wants one.</strong> <code>UPDATED</code> The 18 AUG text schedules a verification test within 90 days of full operation; both 21 SEP drafts test after each phase, and v.2 adds independent testing every year. All of it is taken with the operator's knowledge.<a class="fnref" href="#fs60"><sup>51</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs115"><sup>101</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs116"><sup>102</sup></a> One unannounced property-line reading after commissioning would add what a scheduled test cannot. <strong>Under the 18 AUG text the limit itself already binds at all hours and needs no amendment.</strong></p>
</div>
<p><strong>Novva achieves this with no acoustic barrier at all</strong>: wrought iron fence around the entire property, which provides no meaningful attenuation.<a class="fnref" href="#ff1"><sup>60</sup></a> No one can claim a barrier is doing the work, and this report's own finding that barriers could buy back 5 to 15 dB describes headroom that has not been needed.<a class="fnref" href="#fd0"><sup>43</sup></a></p>
  <h3 id="on-sound-character-the-field-evidence-cuts-against-t">On sound character, the field evidence cuts against this report</h3>
<p><strong>Asked specifically about tonality, the answer was that nothing was annoying about the quality of the sound. It simply sounded like fans running.</strong><a class="fnref" href="#ff1"><sup>60</sup></a> Broadband fan noise is the benign case. A narrow-band whine or hum is what generates complaints, and none was reported.</p>
<p><strong>That matters because tonality is one of the strongest arguments in this report, and a field observation at an operating facility does not support it.</strong> Three limits keep it from settling the question: no spectral measurement was taken, a moving vehicle with an open window masks subtle tonality, and a daytime visit says nothing about nighttime audibility at distance. But it is evidence, it points against the concern, and it belongs in the record even though it weakens a point made elsewhere here.</p>
  <h3 id="what-would-actually-decide-whether-it-is-noticed">What would decide whether it is noticed</h3>
<p><strong>A decibel limit cannot capture the failure mode that generates complaints.</strong> A narrow-band tone is picked out of a quiet background far below the level at which broadband noise registers. Measured studies frame the propagation question. <strong>Beside motorways and railways, where the source sits near the ground, downward refraction raised levels 3 to 4 dB(A) at 150 to 250 m against neutral conditions, and upward refraction cut them by as much as 5 to 10 dB(A)</strong>.<a class="fnref" id="rs135" href="#fs135"><sup>121</sup></a> Two years of measurements 1.1 to 2.0 km from a Swedish wind farm found the median level varied by 6 dBA with refraction conditions, by 14 dBA over snow, and by 14 to 17 dB in the 125 Hz octave band; about 400 and 600 m from the turbines, no clear effect showed.<a class="fnref" id="rs136" href="#fs136"><sup>122</sup></a> Detection rises at night: a year of measurements at homes 0.9 to 3.5 km from three South Australian wind farms found the turbines' amplitude modulation two to five times as often at night as by day, in more than half the night within 1.3 km and about a quarter of it at 3.5 km, least often upwind.<a class="fnref" id="rs137" href="#fs137"><sup>123</sup></a> A receptor at one mile sits at 1,609 m; the nearest homes are about a third of that distance. <code>UPDATED</code> (The 17 AUG edition said refractive focusing tops out around +6 dB. That misread a 1985 study of one wind turbine, which reported focusing of 25 dB or more over terrain.<a class="fnref" id="rs138" href="#fs138"><sup>124</sup></a>)</p>
<p><strong>The wind is mostly protective, and the geometry matters more than this report first stated.</strong> Five years of KSPK ASOS observations, the Iowa Environmental Mesonet archive for station SPK from 24 APR 2021, put the canyon drainage flow moving toward <strong>about 310° at night.</strong><a class="fnref" href="#fd0"><sup>43</sup></a> The nearest homes, in Spanish Fork, bear roughly 255 to 279° from the site, about 30 to 55° off that axis; a second group, newer homes in Mapleton with lot lines about 486 m to the north-northeast, sits 65 to 75° off it. Neither group is squarely downwind after dark.<a class="fnref" href="#fs62"><sup>53</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fd0"><sup>43</sup></a> Homes to the northwest sit closer to the drainage axis than the nearest homes do, which is where nighttime exposure would concentrate if it occurs at all.</p>
<p>At 30 mph, wind through trees, fences and eaves puts local ambient at about <strong>50 to 60 dBA</strong>. <code>UNVERIFIED</code> That is <strong>at least 10 dB above</strong> the top of the facility's modeled 15 to 40 dBA at one mile, and around the top of its range at the nearest homes.<a class="fnref" href="#fd0"><sup>43</sup></a> <code>UPDATED</code> At a mile that is complete masking. The worst case is not the windy nights but the opposite: wind-farm acoustics has known for decades that complaints cluster where the <em>ground</em> is calm while the source region is windy. The condition most likely to make this facility audible is light drainage flow around <strong>2 to 5 m/s under a shallow stable layer</strong>, fast enough to carry sound and benefit from refraction, too slow to mask it. <code>UPDATED</code> The 17 AUG edition called that condition narrow; the recent measurements cited above show night-time refraction can move levels at 1 to 2 km by well over 6 dB.</p>
<div class="callout co-note">
  <div class="lbl">The right ask, and it is not an accusation</div>
  <p>Volition's per-unit figure is Bloom's datasheet figure, and it disclosed that limitation to the Council itself.<a class="fnref" href="#fs3"><sup>3</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs113"><sup>99</sup></a> <code>UPDATED</code> Both 21 SEP drafts now require the tonal and low-frequency analysis and post-construction testing at the nearest existing residences.<a class="fnref" href="#fs115"><sup>101</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs116"><sup>102</sup></a> Publish the property-line study, and put a receptor-located numeric limit with a low-frequency and tonality condition into the agreement, verified after construction rather than modeled before it.</p>
</div>
</section>

<section class="report-section wrap" id="what-would-have-to-be-true-in-both-directions">
  <h2>21. What would have to be true</h2>
