<p><strong>Note the pattern: every adverse outcome is about the ordinance, not the applicant.</strong></p>
  <h3 id="two-of-them-already-happened-once-in-this-exact-buil">Two of them already happened in this building</h3>
<p><strong>Fingerhut built this structure</strong>, and the record is contemporaneous press rather than inference.<a class="fnref" href="#fs20"><sup>17</sup></a> The 04 AUG minutes record the Volition representative identifying the site as <em>"the approximately one-million-square-foot former Fingerhut building."</em><a class="fnref" href="#fs60"><sup>51</sup></a></p>
<p class="tbl-n">TABLE 32</p>
<div class="tw"><table>
<thead><tr><th><strong>Promised</strong></th><th><strong>Delivered</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td><strong><em>"more than 520 employees within two years,"</em> expanding to <em>"as many as 800 workers"</em></strong> <a class="fnref" href="#fs20"><sup>17</sup></a></td><td>250 employees when closure was announced in FEB 2001, the only Fingerhut headcount either article reports <code>UPDATED</code> <a class="fnref" href="#fs19"><sup>16</sup></a></td></tr>
<tr><td>~$10.4M in new wages yearly; <strong>$5.1M in property taxes within 12 years</strong>; $163.7M Utah spend by 2004 <a class="fnref" href="#fs19"><sup>16</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs20"><sup>17</sup></a></td><td>Closure announced FEB 2001; <em>"about 100 of its 250 employees lost jobs"</em>; by OCT 2001 <em>"all of the workers are now gone"</em> <a class="fnref" href="#fs19"><sup>16</sup></a></td></tr>
<tr><td>A <strong>$70 million</strong> facility as Fingerhut's Western hub <a class="fnref" href="#fs19"><sup>16</sup></a></td><td><em>"the distribution center never fully opened"</em> <a class="fnref" href="#fs19"><sup>16</sup></a></td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>n/a</strong></td><td>The city had already spent $220,000 straightening Powerhouse Road, which serves this site, for the roughly 60 trucks a day Fingerhut expected at a plant that never fully opened <a class="fnref" href="#fs19"><sup>16</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs20"><sup>17</sup></a></td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p><em>Deseret News</em>, <strong>20 OCT 2003</strong> carries the 520/800 employment figures and the <strong>163 acres</strong>; <strong>30 OCT 2001</strong> carries the $70M, <em>"never fully opened,"</em> and the 100-of-250 layoffs.<a class="fnref" href="#fs19"><sup>16</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs20"><sup>17</sup></a> <code>UPDATED</code> The $220,000 road figure, the 60 trucks a day and the $10.4 million wage projection appear in both articles; the rest appears in only one.</p>
<div class="callout co-alert">
  <div class="lbl">⚠ Three limits</div>
  <p>Do not say "Fingerhut got incentives and there was no clawback." The only documented public money is the <strong>$220,000 road expenditure</strong>;<a class="fnref" href="#fs19"><sup>16</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs20"><sup>17</sup></a> anything beyond it is <strong>UNVERIFIED</strong>. No clawback failure is established: the 2001 article says only that <em>"officials are rewriting the contract with Fingerhut to make sure the city is protected when a new owner takes over,"</em> which implies a contract existed, not that recapture failed.<a class="fnref" href="#fs19"><sup>16</sup></a> And this is not evidence about Volition: different company, different industry, and a market collapse. It is evidence about the building, the road and the city's exposure, and about how a promise of hundreds of jobs and millions in tax base at this address has resolved once before.</p>
</div>
<p><strong>One conflict, now settled:</strong> the 2003 article places the former plant on <strong>163 acres</strong> against the assessor's <strong>69.478</strong>.<a class="fnref" href="#fs20"><sup>17</sup></a> <strong>Three independent records agree on the smaller figure</strong>: the Assessor at 69.478641, the broker's flyer at 69.9, and this review's own computation of the state parcel polygon at 69.50.<a class="fnref" href="#fs12"><sup>10</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs64"><sup>55</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fd0"><sup>43</sup></a> <strong>The 163-acre figure describes something other than this parcel</strong>, most likely a larger original assemblage, and should not be used for buffer or expansion arguments.</p>
  <h3 id="where-the-national-record-actually-lands">Where the national record lands</h3>
<p><strong>The closest structural analogue is xAI "Colossus," Memphis</strong>, per <em>NAACP v. X.AI Corp.</em>, No. 3:26-cv-00074 (N.D. Miss., filed 14 APR 2026): <em>"Neither xAI nor CTC Property LLC applied for or received any air permits prior to constructing and beginning to operate these sixteen gas turbines"</em> (¶197), reaching <em>"thirty-five turbines for a total generating capacity of 421.4 MW"</em> by 31 MAR 2025 (¶198).<a class="fnref" id="rs106" href="#fs106"><sup>92</sup></a></p>
<p><strong>Why it transfers:</strong> a self-generating AI data center, the configuration Spanish Fork is being asked to authorize by right, where the failure was process rather than technology. Why it does not: xAI ran combustion turbines. Its unpermitted Southaven, Mississippi turbines have a potential to emit of about 1,700 tons of NOx a year from 495 MW, about 0.78 lb/MWh, roughly 260 times the rate in Volition's draft permit. These are allegations in a filed complaint, not adjudicated findings.<a class="fnref" href="#fs106"><sup>92</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fd0"><sup>43</sup></a> The transferable point is narrower. <code>UPDATED</code> At Colossus 1 in Memphis the turbines ran without air permits until the operator obtained a permit for a different configuration, with an 87 ton-a-year NOx limit. The NAACP's Clean Air Act citizen suit targets the second plant, 27 turbines and 495 MW at Southaven.<a class="fnref" href="#fs106"><sup>92</sup></a> Where a federal statute supplies a hook, residents can sue. In Spanish Fork, air is DAQ's and siting is the city's. Under the 18 AUG text, whose only scheduled post-construction check for fuel cells like these is a noise test, the city has few triggers to pull; v.2 of the 21 SEP drafts adds yearly independent testing of noise, water and air.<a class="fnref" href="#fs116"><sup>102</sup></a></p>
<p><strong>The Utah record cuts the other way.</strong> KSL measured <strong>~70 dB about one block from Aligned's West Jordan campus</strong> (10 JUL 2026), with residents describing loud blowers that Aligned attributes to backup generators it says sit mostly idle.<a class="fnref" id="rs32" href="#fs32"><sup>26</sup></a> <code>UPDATED</code> This review found no enforcement action, notice of violation or citation against Aligned, and no documented complaint of any kind about Novva. Residents complain; nobody has been cited. Expect Volition to make that point, and it is fair.</p>
  <h3 id="the-broken-siting-promises-narrative-does-not-surviv">The "broken siting promises" narrative does not survive verification</h3>
<p>A public commenter alleged a documented national pattern of data centers breaking siting commitments. A dedicated search across Northern Virginia, whose Loudoun County holds the world's largest concentration of data centers,<a class="fnref" href="#fs99"><sup>85</sup></a> found no agency finding and no adjudicated violation against any named operator on any of those grounds. Every noise case traced there ends one of three ways: the county measured and found no exceedance, the conduct was legally exempt, or the facility held a valid permit.</p>
<p>At <strong>Vantage II in Sterling</strong>, the closest Virginia analogue, with eight gas turbines running 24/7, a county supervisor citing staff readings said the average <em>"has never exceeded 55 dBA"</em> at the adjacent residential property.<a class="fnref" href="#fs99"><sup>85</sup></a> <code>UPDATED</code> At <strong>AWS / Great Oak</strong>, a <strong>1989 exemption for air-conditioning equipment</strong> meant, in a homeowners' coalition's words, that <em>"the County has no legal ability to control noise at any level from data center cooling equipment,"</em> though a resident's meter read 67.4 dB.<a class="fnref" id="rs139" href="#fs139"><sup>125</sup></a> And <strong>"Chesterton" is not a Virginia case</strong>: a $1.3 billion proposal in Chesterton, Indiana was withdrawn on 10 JUN 2024, before any approval.<a class="fnref" id="rs140" href="#fs140"><sup>126</sup></a> (The 17 AUG edition cited Spanish Fork's own Title 15 for all three, which supports none of them.)</p>
<p><strong>The one fully adjudicated case points somewhere else entirely.</strong> The Prince William Digital Gateway rezonings, about 1,760 acres and 22.2 million sq ft, were declared void ab initio because the County Board violated Virginia's public-notice statute: a legal ad was drafted, never confirmed by the newspaper's deadline, and never ran.<a class="fnref" id="rs22" href="#fs22"><sup>19</sup></a></p>
<div class="callout co-note">
  <p><em>"Because the Board violated the advertising rules in Code § 15.2-2204(A)… we affirm the trial court's judgment declaring the Compass, DG South, and DG North rezonings… <strong>void ab initio.</strong>"</em> Court of Appeals of Virginia, 31 MAR 2026<a class="fnref" href="#fs22"><sup>19</sup></a></p>
</div>
<p><strong>The project is dead</strong>: finality came from <strong>QTS withdrawing its Supreme Court filing on 02 JUL 2026</strong>, not from the opinion.<a class="fnref" id="rs141" href="#fs141"><sup>127</sup></a> A 22.2-million-square-foot approval was undone by a newspaper ad that did not run.<a class="fnref" href="#fs22"><sup>19</sup></a></p>
<p><strong>Two conclusions follow, and they pull against each other.</strong></p>
<ol>
  <li><strong>The adversarial case against operators is weaker than the public conversation suggests.</strong> Where noise is measured by an authority with jurisdiction, exceedances mostly are not found.</li>
  <li>The failures that get remedied are failures of local process. Digital Gateway died over notice.<a class="fnref" href="#fs22"><sup>19</sup></a> Great Oak went 18 months without relief, most of it under a county noise ordinance that exempted cooling equipment from its nighttime limits.<a class="fnref" href="#fs139"><sup>125</sup></a> Vantage's turbines entered without additional review because Loudoun's zoning definition swept <em>"associated utility infrastructure"</em> inside the term "data center",<a class="fnref" href="#fs99"><sup>85</sup></a> structurally similar to what §15.3.24 does in combining data center and generation into one administratively approved use.</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>The lesson is not "watch the applicant." It is that the ordinance is the only thing that will still be here in ten years.</strong></p>
</section>

<section class="report-section wrap" id="four-arguments-not-to-make-because-the-evidence-refu">
  <h2>22. Five arguments not to make</h2>
<p class="lead">An adversarial review should disarm bad arguments as well as find good ones. Each of these circulates in data center opposition, and <strong>each would be corrected in the room.</strong></p>
<p><strong>1. "Data centers lower nearby property values."</strong> The verifiable literature does not support it, and the strongest study finds the opposite (§16).<a class="fnref" href="#fs69"><sup>62</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs70"><sup>63</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs21"><sup>18</sup></a> The legitimate version of this concern is a question, not a claim: no study has measured a data center paired with a 393 MW on-site power plant.<a class="fnref" href="#fs1"><sup>1</sup></a> Ask which coefficient this facility fits. Do not assert the answer.</p>
<p><strong>2. "$120 billion in off-balance-sheet AI financing, per the Bank for International Settlements."</strong> <strong>Misattributed.</strong> The figure is from the <strong>Financial Times, 24 DEC 2025</strong>; the BIS Quarterly Review feature on AI financing returns <strong>zero matches</strong> for it.<a class="fnref" id="rs100" href="#fs100"><sup>86</sup></a> BIS economists did call these structures <strong>"shadow borrowing"</strong>, under the usual disclaimer that the views are the authors' own.<a class="fnref" href="#fs100"><sup>86</sup></a> <strong>Moody's $662 billion, the five largest hyperscalers' data center lease commitments not yet on their balance sheets, is the better number.</strong><a class="fnref" id="rs142" href="#fs142"><sup>128</sup></a> The concern is real; the citation is not, and attributing it to BIS invites a correction that discredits the point.</p>
<p><strong>3. "The project will be abandoned and stick the city with stranded infrastructure."</strong> This review found no documented case of a US municipality left with stranded infrastructure by a data center bankruptcy. Kearney, Nebraska's city manager stated Compute North's payments <em>"are paid in full,"</em><a class="fnref" id="rs143" href="#fs143"><sup>129</sup></a> and Cyxtera's facilities were bought and kept running.<a class="fnref" id="rs144" href="#fs144"><sup>130</sup></a></p>
<div class="callout co-note">
  <div class="lbl">A negative finding, not a clean bill of health</div>
  <p>Sungard, Core Scientific, Applied Digital, and TeraWulf were never researched. The correct statement is <em>"no such case was found,"</em> not <em>"no such case exists."</em></p>
</div>
<p><strong>4. "Ratepayers will subsidize this."</strong> The critique applies to grid-connected large loads, whose grid upgrades can fall to ratepayers.<a class="fnref" id="rs101" href="#fs101"><sup>87</sup></a> The islanded design answers it rather than illustrating it: the 18 AUG text requires permanent electrical isolation, and both 21 SEP drafts state that approval does not obligate the city or its utility customers to subsidize project-specific energy infrastructure.<a class="fnref" href="#fs60"><sup>51</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs115"><sup>101</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs116"><sup>102</sup></a></p>
<p><strong>5. "The fuel cells and the compute load will expose neighbors to electromagnetic fields."</strong> <strong>The distances defeat this before the technology matters.</strong> <code>UPDATED</code> An NIEHS booklet, reproducing Bonneville Power Administration values calculated 1 m above ground, puts the mean field from a 230 kV line at <strong>57.5 mG beneath the line, 19.5 mG at the approximate edge of the right-of-way, 7.1 mG at 100 ft and 1.8 mG at 200 ft</strong>, and says that at <strong>300 ft</strong>, at average demand, many lines produce fields similar to typical household background.<a class="fnref" id="rs83" href="#fs83"><sup>36</sup></a> <strong>The nearest residential lot line is about 1,300 ft away</strong>, and ICNIRP's public reference level for 60 Hz magnetic fields is <strong>200 µT, or 2,000 mG</strong>.<a class="fnref" href="#fs62"><sup>53</sup></a><a class="fnref" id="rs84" href="#fs84"><sup>37</sup></a> For scale, the same federal booklet puts <strong>the median hair dryer at 300 mG at six inches</strong>.<a class="fnref" href="#fs83"><sup>36</sup></a></p>
<p><strong>And this facility has no transmission line at all.</strong> The 18 AUG text's O.5 requires permanent electrical isolation with no interconnection, parallel operation or export, and both 21 SEP drafts define the generation facility as operating in electrical isolation, so <strong>the grid-tie infrastructure that produces the fields in every one of these studies is absent by design.</strong> Only ordinary office and maintenance portions may connect to the city system.<a class="fnref" href="#fs60"><sup>51</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs115"><sup>101</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs116"><sup>102</sup></a> <code>UPDATED</code></p>
<p><strong>The area's existing 60 Hz environment is set by transmission the facility has nothing to do with.</strong> Four 345 kV circuits, two 138 kV circuits and nine 115 kV lines end at the Spanish Fork substation under a mile north of the site, and more 115 kV segments cross the area.<a class="fnref" href="#fs87"><sup>40</sup></a> <code>UPDATED</code> <strong>A residence across the street from a heavily loaded transmission line sits in a measurably higher power-frequency field than a home 1,500 ft or more from a facility with no line at all.</strong><a class="fnref" href="#fs83"><sup>36</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fd0"><sup>43</sup></a> <strong>State that carefully if at all.</strong> <em>"You are already exposed to more"</em> reads as dismissive and invites the reply <em>"so you concede there is exposure."</em> <strong>The affirmative version needs no comparison: there is no transmission line, and a few hundred feet from a line the field is at household levels.</strong> A knowledgeable objector would raise one technical point: <strong>magnetic field scales with current, electric field with voltage</strong>, so a line's kV rating does not by itself establish its magnetic field, and a heavily loaded 115 kV circuit can exceed a lightly loaded 345 kV one.<a class="fnref" href="#fs83"><sup>36</sup></a></p>
<p><strong>The professional literature on data centers and EMF runs in the opposite direction from the concern.</strong> A 2025 article in CIGRE's journal <em>ELECTRA</em> is about <strong>transmission lines interfering with data centers</strong>, not data centers emitting toward neighbors, and the design reference it cites, TIA-942-C Annex F, recommends keeping the data hall itself <strong>below 37.5 mG</strong> to protect the equipment.<a class="fnref" id="rs85" href="#fs85"><sup>38</sup></a> <strong>That is an immunity specification for servers, not an emission limit for residents.</strong></p>
<div class="callout co-note">
  <div class="lbl">The narrow version of this that is real, and why it still does not reach a neighbor</div>
  <p>Power electronics differ from rotating machines: an inverter switches at kilohertz and produces harmonics a diesel alternator does not, while a diesel set produces voltage transients on load steps that an inverter does not. IEEE 519 caps voltage total harmonic distortion at <strong>5% at medium-voltage points of common coupling</strong>, and IEC 61000-6-4 governs conducted and radiated emissions for industrial equipment.<a class="fnref" id="rs86" href="#fs86"><sup>39</sup></a> <strong>With no point of common coupling, none of it can propagate onto the municipal system.</strong> §15.3.24 contains no EMF or EMC standard, and no regulator supplies one for a non-interconnected facility, but unlike the missing thermal standard <strong>this gap has no receptor</strong>. <strong>Do not spend Council time on it.</strong></p>
</div>
  <h3 id="and-one-argument-the-record-hands-the-council-fully-">One argument the record hands the Council</h3>
<p><strong>Columbia Law School's Sabin Center for Climate Change Law recommends financial assurance, such as bonds, for data center decommissioning, and names two local ordinances: Susquehanna County and Smithfield Township, Pennsylvania.</strong><a class="fnref" href="#fs101"><sup>87</sup></a> <code>UPDATED</code> In the 18 AUG text the decommissioning provision is the weakest in the ordinance; both 21 SEP drafts now require financial assurance.<a class="fnref" href="#fs60"><sup>51</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs115"><sup>101</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs116"><sup>102</sup></a> Volition has filed no Form D under its current or former name, against a $4 billion build.<a class="fnref" href="#fs98"><sup>84</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs103"><sup>89</sup></a> The applicant told the Council on 18 AUG that decommissioning could cost more than $30 million.<a class="fnref" href="#fs90"><sup>76</sup></a></p>
<div class="callout co-note">
  <p>Require a decommissioning bond or escrow sized to actual removal cost, reviewed on a fixed cycle, <strong>running with the land rather than with the applicant.</strong> Susquehanna County, PA requires decommissioning funds equal to net decommissioning cost after salvage, plus 10%, as a bond or other financial assurance delivered before construction begins.<a class="fnref" href="#fs101"><sup>87</sup></a> Smithfield Township, PA requires a decommissioning agreement that includes <em>"a bond or letter of credit."</em><a class="fnref" id="rs111" href="#fs111"><sup>97</sup></a> <code>UPDATED</code> The applicant's 01 SEP presentation offers a restoration estimate refreshed every three years with City step-in and lien rights, and the City Attorney described the draft development agreement as including financial assurance for decommissioning. Neither names a bond or its size, and neither is executed. <code>APPLICANT ONLY</code><a class="fnref" href="#fs91"><sup>77</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs90"><sup>76</sup></a></p>
</div>
<p><strong>Both drafts now require financial assurance, but v.2 caps it at the owner's own estimate and ties it to hazardous materials on site at cessation. Sizing it to actual removal cost is where the Council can be handed a solution instead of a problem</strong>, and it survives every optimistic assumption about Volition, because it matters most if Volition cannot or does not pay.</p>
</section>

<section class="report-section wrap" id="where-this-report-is-incomplete">
  <h2>23. Where this report is incomplete</h2>
<p class="lead"><strong>Three national cases were run down and each is narrower than its reputation.</strong> Cheyenne is real but is not about Meta's operations: the Board of Public Utilities issued a <strong>Notice of Significant Noncompliance on 02 JUL 2026</strong> to Goat Systems LLC, whose address is 1 Meta Way, over <em>Cupriavidus gilardii</em> contamination tied to construction-phase fill-and-flush discharge, with a <strong>$10,000 administrative fine</strong>. Goat Systems paid the fine under protest and appealed on 13 JUL, disputing which entity held the permit. No ruling was found as of 21 SEP.<a class="fnref" id="rs145" href="#fs145"><sup>131</sup></a> <code>UPDATED</code> Chandler and Mesa are negatives: this review found no citation in either, and both cities responded with prospective ordinances.<a class="fnref" id="rs146" href="#fs146"><sup>132</sup></a> The Dalles was a public-records fight, not a water-use violation: the city sued to overturn an order to disclose Google's water data, then settled before any ruling.<a class="fnref" id="rs147" href="#fs147"><sup>133</sup></a> No water violation was found against Google there. Do not cite The Dalles as an operator violation.</p>
<p><strong>Unverified and not to be stated to the Council:</strong> the public commenter's broken-promise claims beyond the Northern Virginia set, which was retrieved and largely refuted; and <strong>a second public commenter's BIS / $120B claim</strong>, traced and misattributed: the figure is the Financial Times'.<a class="fnref" href="#fs100"><sup>86</sup></a></p>
<p><strong>Known limits on the numbers here:</strong></p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>The noise model's source term is the weakest link.</strong> <code>UPDATED</code> KSL's ~70 dB "about a block away" at Aligned is a different facility with different equipment, so it cannot test the Bloom source term.<a class="fnref" href="#fs32"><sup>26</sup></a> The finite-line and ISO 3744 treatments are the reason to read every noise figure here as a lower bound, and the property-line study as the only authority.<a class="fnref" href="#fd0"><sup>43</sup></a></li>
  <li><strong>The per-truck comparison depends on an assumed 100,000 mi/truck-year</strong> that appears in no document. On the 04 AUG deck's own basis, about 3,500 trucks a month at a 10-mile round trip, the comparison is far worse for the claim.<a class="fnref" href="#fs113"><sup>99</sup></a> Without a stated basis, the claim cannot be evaluated. The fleet-level figure (2.2 to 2.3×) tests the slide only against its own numbers and is not a site comparison. <code>APPLICANT ONLY</code> <code>UPDATED</code><a class="fnref" href="#fd0"><sup>43</sup></a></li>
  <li><strong>No traffic count for this building exists in the public record</strong>, by the city, the applicant or this review. Every truck figure in this report is <strong>calculated here from published ITE rates against the verified 1,054,000 sq ft</strong>, not measured and not taken from the applicant.<a class="fnref" href="#fs88"><sup>41</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs89"><sup>42</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs64"><sup>55</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fd0"><sup>43</sup></a> Present truck volume is unmeasured, and the applicant's own figures for it disagree, so the comparison throughout is the proposed data center against the fully tenanted warehouse, not against current conditions.<a class="fnref" href="#fs103"><sup>89</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs91"><sup>77</sup></a> <strong>Two limits on the calculation:</strong> ITE rates are national averages, and the highest rest on few sites, the parcel hub and fulfillment rates on one site each in ITE's 2016 study; and <strong>the tenant is unknown</strong>.<a class="fnref" href="#fs117"><sup>103</sup></a> <code>UPDATED</code> Because no count exists for this building or a comparable Utah site, this revision states each warehouse type at its highest published ITE rate and takes the busiest use the zoning allows by right, a parcel hub, as the maximum, rather than the fulfillment use the building was built for.<a class="fnref" href="#fs13"><sup>11</sup></a></li>
  <li><strong>The depreciation curve applies R884-24P-33, a <em>personal property</em> schedule.</strong> A building retrofit is real property and depreciates differently.<a class="fnref" href="#fs17"><sup>14</sup></a> The pro forma request stands; the curve is illustrative.</li>
  <li><strong>The CO2 method's agreement with Bloom's own datasheet is circular</strong>, same stoichiometry, same efficiency range.<a class="fnref" href="#fs31"><sup>25</sup></a> The independent check is agreement with Novva's regulator-issued Utah permit: within 1% at the engine's published 45.5 to 45.9% efficiency, once both figures are in metric tons.<a class="fnref" href="#fs47"><sup>31</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fd0"><sup>43</sup></a> <code>UPDATED</code> (The 17 AUG edition claimed 1% agreement, comparing a metric figure with a short-ton one.)</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Emissions at the proposed nameplate and the cap, in a high operating case.</strong> At the CARB DG certification standard of 0.07 lb/MWh, the rate a unit must meet to be certified and <strong>23× Bloom's published 0.003 lb/MWh</strong>, at an 85% capacity factor: <code>UPDATED</code><a class="fnref" href="#fs3"><sup>3</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs46"><sup>30</sup></a></p>
<p>250 MW × 8,760 h × 0.85 × 0.07 lb/MWh ÷ 2,000 = 65 tons/yr<br>393.25 MW × 8,760 h × 0.85 × 0.07 lb/MWh ÷ 2,000 = 102 tons/yr<br>450 MW × 8,760 h × 0.85 × 0.07 lb/MWh ÷ 2,000 = 117 tons/yr</p>
<p>for scale, at Bloom's published 0.003 lb/MWh, 450 MW gives 5.0 tons/yr<a class="fnref" href="#fd0"><sup>43</sup></a></p>
<p><strong>Both the proposed nameplate and the ordinance cap cross the 100 tpy NNSR major-source threshold</strong> for this Marginal ozone area.<a class="fnref" href="#fs15"><sup>13</sup></a> ⚠ <strong>But be careful with this one:</strong> it requires a unit emitting at the CARB certification standard, 23× Bloom's published rate, so it is an argument about what the <em>ordinance</em> would permit a future applicant to build, not a prediction about this equipment.<a class="fnref" href="#fs3"><sup>3</sup></a> <code>UPDATED</code> The 18 AUG text also admits Controlled-Emission equipment up to 0.30 lb/MWh under Enhanced Review; at that rate 100 tpy is crossed at about 90 MW at 85% load, or about 76 MW at full-time potential to emit, and 450 MW reaches about 503 tpy.<a class="fnref" href="#fd0"><sup>43</sup></a> <code>UPDATED</code> A potential-to-emit cap in the code is what forecloses this, and the republished 18 AUG text adds one: O.7 caps facility-wide NOx potential to emit below the major-source threshold by federally enforceable limits in the DAQ order. Both 21 SEP drafts keep that ceiling by requiring a minor-source permit.<a class="fnref" href="#fs60"><sup>51</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs115"><sup>101</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs116"><sup>102</sup></a> (The 17 AUG edition, written against the earlier text, said the Justification Statement wrongly claimed such a cap existed.)</p>
<div class="callout co-alert">
  <div class="lbl">⚠ On offsets, be precise</div>
  <p><code>UPDATED</code> R307-421, <em>Permits: PM10 Offset Requirements in Salt Lake County and Utah County</em>, works in two steps. R307-421-2 applies to new NOx or SO2 sources located in Utah County, and uses a modeled-impact test of 1.0 µg/m³ annual or 3.0 µg/m³ 24-hour only to bring in sources outside the county. R307-421-3 then requires offsets from <strong>25 tons a year</strong> (1:1) and <strong>50 tons a year</strong> (1.2:1). The rule has governed Utah County since EPA's PM10 redesignation on 27 MAR 2020.<a class="fnref" href="#fs45"><sup>29</sup></a> <strong>At 5.17 tpy NOx and 0.01 tpy SO2, no offset applies to this facility</strong>, whatever the modeling shows.<a class="fnref" href="#fs1"><sup>1</sup></a> The nearest comparable, Novva, at 51.28 tpy, was required to obtain <strong>47 tpy of NOx emission reduction credits</strong> under the rule.<a class="fnref" href="#fs47"><sup>31</sup></a> The 17 AUG edition said the rule contained no tonnage figure and warned against asserting a 50 tpy threshold; both statements were wrong.</p>
</div>
<p><strong>Two things the ordinance is sometimes accused of, which it does not do:</strong> it does not permit unlimited merchant power plants (generation must be <em>"scaled to serve"</em> on-site load, and the definition bars offsite sale, in the 18 AUG text's O.2 and in both 21 SEP drafts), and it does not lack a receptor-located noise limit (the 18 AUG text's O.6 sets 55 dBA at any residential zone boundary; the 21 SEP drafts drop that figure and rely on Chapter 9.32, which applies the most restrictive limit at the boundary between land-use categories).<a class="fnref" href="#fs60"><sup>51</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs115"><sup>101</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs116"><sup>102</sup></a></p>
<p><code>UPDATED</code> <strong>One fiscal item the 17 AUG edition raised does not hold.</strong> Spanish Fork levies the municipal energy sales and use tax at 6%, but Utah Code 10-1-305(2)(b)(iv) requires every such ordinance to exempt the sale or use of taxable energy whose primary use is producing taxable energy. Gas used to generate the facility's own electricity falls inside that exemption, so it is not a revenue source for the city.<a class="fnref" id="rs102" href="#fs102"><sup>88</sup></a> The 17 AUG edition called it plausibly comparable to the city's property tax share, on a stale 250 MW, 10 Bcf basis; at 393.25 MW and 75% load the gas is about 14.5 to 17.8 Bcf a year, and the exemption applies regardless.<a class="fnref" href="#fd0"><sup>43</sup></a></p>
</section>

<section class="report-section wrap" id="about-the-author">
  <h2>24. About the author</h2>
<div class="author">
  <p><strong>Brady Redfearn</strong> is a technical product leader with a PhD in Systems Engineering from Virginia Tech and twenty years building and governing complex technical systems. <strong>The subject matter here is his field.</strong></p>
  <p><strong>The measurements and the modeling both come out of the degree.</strong> The first two years of his PhD were in industrial hygiene and safety, the discipline of assessing occupational and environmental exposure, alongside coursework in human factors and ergonomics. Noise, heat stress, and electromagnetic fields are its three classical subjects, and they are exactly what §20 and §22 examine. That training is why the 14 AUG field work was run as a controlled transect rather than a walk-around, and why every reading taken on a phone rather than a calibrated meter is marked <code>UNCALIBRATED</code> and treated as corroboration only. Systems Engineering supplies the rest, modeling a system end to end and testing whether its stated behavior matches its physical constraints: an emissions calculation carried at the permit's own method, an acoustic model inverted from a manufacturer's source term and reported with its uncertainty intact, a thermal balance, and a property tax chain run against the state's own depreciation schedules.</p>
  <p><strong>He works on the demand this facility exists to serve.</strong> He worked on components of IBM Watson and has been building AI products since 2012, through the years when training and inference went from a research line item to something utilities plan capacity around, and has worked on AI policy and implementation as a contractor for the Department of Commerce. A data center of this size is a physical expression of that demand. Knowing what the load is, how it varies, and which assumptions about utilization and refresh cycles are realistic is a working requirement of that job rather than research undertaken for this report.</p>
  <p><strong>The infrastructure side is familiar ground.</strong> His BS is in IT and Cybersecurity from Brigham Young University, the discipline covering power, cooling, redundancy, and network topology inside a data hall. He has worked on industrial IoT with Idaho National Laboratory and written research reports and cybersecurity policy for the Department of Homeland Security. Industrial control systems, islanded generation, and the posture of a facility with no grid interconnection are not unfamiliar territory.</p>
  <p><strong>Evaluating technical claims under adversarial review is the day job.</strong> He spent nearly seven years at a defense technology contractor, directing a $45M portfolio across 14 concurrent programs and an organization of 12 across five teams, including adversarial AI risk research at the Army Cyber Institute; before that, he worked on the human-AI interaction model for a powered exoskeleton at U.S. Special Operations Command. He holds an active DoD Secret clearance. <code>UPDATED</code> That work is the routine reading of permits, specifications and contracts to determine what they actually authorize rather than what they are described as authorizing. This report applies that method to a municipal ordinance.</p>
</div>
<div class="callout co-insight">
  <div class="lbl">Statement of interest</div>
  <p>The author is a Spanish Fork resident and lives approximately one mile from the subject site. <code>UPDATED</code> He spoke in support of the amendment at the 18 AUG and 01 SEP 2026 Council meetings, on the strength of this research.<a class="fnref" href="#fs90"><sup>76</sup></a><a class="fnref" href="#fs118"><sup>104</sup></a> He has no financial interest in Volition Industries, in any competing applicant, or in the subject parcel, and receives no compensation from any party to this proceeding. This report was prepared independently and at no one's direction.</p>
</div>
</section>

