    <li id="fs134"><a class="fnback" href="#rs134">&#8617;</a> <strong>WPR, "Microsoft sued over noise complaints at new Mount Pleasant data center facility," 02 JUL 2026</strong>: three Sturtevant residents filed a class action in the U.S. District Court for the Eastern District of Wisconsin on 01 JUL 2026; the complaint calls the noise "not only excessive, but consistent and pervasive" and attributes it to "diesel generators and heating, ventilation, and air conditioning (HVAC) systems, including chillers, cooling towers, air-handling units, and condenser fans." <strong>Allegations, not findings.</strong> Added in the {{ revised }} revision <a href="https://www.wpr.org/news/microsoft-sued-noise-complaints-at-new-mount-pleasant-data-center" target="_blank" rel="noopener">wpr.org</a></li>
    <li id="fs135"><a class="fnback" href="#rs135">&#8617;</a> <strong>Dieter Hohenwarter, Erich Mursch-Radlgruber and Christian Kirisits, "S-shaped dependence of the sound pressure level in outdoor propagation on the effective sound speed gradient," <em>Acta Acustica</em> 6, 13 (2022)</strong>, open access (CC BY 4.0), published 12 APR 2022. Measurements at motorways and railways, 100 to 500 m from the source: "In the distance range between 150 m and 250 m, the meteorological influences cause a level increase of 3–4 dB(A) for downward refracted sound rays (favourable sound propagation) compared to a situation without meteorological influence," and "a maximum sound level attenuation of 5–10 dB(A)" for upward refraction. Figures from the published abstract; the journal's site refuses automated checks and opens in a browser. Added in the {{ revised }} revision <a href="https://doi.org/10.1051/aacus/2022005" target="_blank" rel="noopener">doi.org (Acta Acustica)</a></li>
    <li id="fs136"><a class="fnback" href="#rs136">&#8617;</a> <strong>Olof Öhlund, <em>Wind Turbine Sound Propagation in the Atmospheric Boundary Layer</em></strong>, licentiate thesis, Department of Earth Sciences, Uppsala University, 2014; its measurement paper was published as Öhlund and Larsson, "Meteorological effects on wind turbine sound propagation," <em>Applied Acoustics</em> 89 (2015), 34–41. Two years of continuous acoustic and meteorological measurements at two Swedish wind farms: 1,060 to 1,970 m from the turbines the median level varied by 6 dBA across refraction conditions without snow cover and 14 dBA with it, and by 14 dB (17 dB over snow) in the 125 Hz octave band; at about 400 and 600 m no clear meteorological dependence was seen (abstract and pp. 21 to 23). Added in the {{ revised }} revision <a href="https://www.diva-portal.org/smash/get/diva2:715762/FULLTEXT02.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">diva-portal.org (Uppsala University)</a></li>
    <li id="fs137"><a class="fnback" href="#rs137">&#8617;</a> <strong>Phuc D. Nguyen, Kristy L. Hansen, Peter Catcheside, Colin H. Hansen and Branko Zajamsek, "Long-term quantification and characterisation of wind farm noise amplitude modulation," <em>Measurement</em> 182 (2021), 109678</strong>; open copy on arXiv (2206.02543). A year of measurements at three homes 890 m, 1.3 km and 3.5 km from three wind farms in South Australia: nighttime prevalence of amplitude modulation "approximately 2 to 5 times higher than the daytime prevalence"; within 1.3 km it occurred during more than 50% of the night against 25% of the day, and at 3.5 km during about 25% of the night against 3% of the day; it was least common upwind. Added in the {{ revised }} revision <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2206.02543" target="_blank" rel="noopener">arxiv.org</a></li>
    <li id="fs138"><a class="fnback" href="#rs138">&#8617;</a> <strong>N. D. Kelley, H. E. McKenna, R. R. Hemphill and others, <em>Acoustic noise associated with the MOD-1 wind turbine: its source, impact, and control</em>, Solar Energy Research Institute, SERI/TR-635-1166, FEB 1985</strong>. <strong>Cited as history only.</strong> §6.4 (pp. 168 to 171): amplification from "thin" atmospheric layers "probably did not exceed 6 dB," while refraction and terrain focusing produced "far-field enhancements in excess of 25 dB," confirmed by measurement; §9.3 (pp. 225 to 226): the focusing "is capable of raising local incident levels to more than 25 dB above the figure expected from the spherical spreading of the source alone." Added in the {{ revised }} revision <a href="https://www.osti.gov/biblio/5875386" target="_blank" rel="noopener">osti.gov</a></li>
    <li id="fs139"><a class="fnback" href="#rs139">&#8617;</a> <strong>Great Oak reporting.</strong> <em>DataCenterDynamics</em>, "Prince William residents complain of 'catastrophic noise' from data centers," 19 JUL 2022: the county "specifically excluded air-conditioning noise from regulation in a 1989 ordinance," and the residents' groups wrote that "the County has no legal ability to control noise at any level from data center cooling equipment." <em>Prince William Times</em>, "Residents turn up the volume on data center noise complaints," 31 AUG 2022: a Great Oak resident's reading of 67.4 decibels; an ordinance that exempts data center air-conditioning units from nighttime limits; and AWS's statement that a third-party study found average noise below the daytime limit. <em>Prince William Times</em>, "Supervisors crack down on data center noise but enforcement remains unclear," 02 MAR 2023: the Board removed the nighttime exemption for non-residential air-conditioning. <em>Prince William Times</em>, "Amazon tones down its data center noise after residents sound the alarm," 23 OCT 2023: 18 months after residents began to hear the noise, Amazon's engineering work had cut it by 10 decibels. Added in the {{ revised }} revision <a href="https://www.datacenterdynamics.com/en/news/prince-william-residents-complain-of-catastrophic-noise-from-data-centers/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">datacenterdynamics.com</a> <a href="https://www.princewilliamtimes.com/news/residents-turn-up-the-volume-on-data-center-noise-complaints/article_08a1c042-293b-11ed-8f1c-07644ca3f58b.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">princewilliamtimes.com (AUG 2022)</a> <a href="https://www.princewilliamtimes.com/news/supervisors-crack-down-on-data-center-noise-but-enforcement-remains-unclear/article_1af31604-b93e-11ed-ae94-138163f1635b.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">princewilliamtimes.com (MAR 2023)</a> <a href="https://www.princewilliamtimes.com/news/amazon-tones-down-its-data-center-noise-after-residents-sound-the-alarm/article_b81154ea-5178-59cb-ac3b-82228a2a52cf.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">princewilliamtimes.com (OCT 2023)</a></li>
